1
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:06,110
("Without intending it,
without knowing it,")

2
00:00:06,110 --> 00:00:08,040
("they advance the interests of society,")

3
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:09,780
("led by an invisible hand.")

4
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:11,450
(By Adam Smith,
from "The Theory of Moral Sentiments")

5
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:14,450
When everyone acts according to
their own self-interest,

6
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:18,550
it eventually leads to balance and order.

7
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:23,760
This process is known
as the invisible hand.

8
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:29,130
To all the residents
moving into Community Town, welcome.

9
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:33,100
Community Town consists of many groups.

10
00:00:33,430 --> 00:00:36,170
Groups categorized by color
will share the same living space.

11
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:38,840
Groups must trade and negotiate
with one another...

12
00:00:39,070 --> 00:00:41,040
to fulfill their respective needs.

13
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:45,410
Each group has a specialized role.

14
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:48,850
Group White determines the prize money.

15
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:51,950
Group Blue manages survival.

16
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:54,860
Group Red controls the games.

17
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:58,690
Group Black distributes
food and information.

18
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:02,760
In order for all residents
to survive the full nine days...

19
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,970
and split the prize money,
200,000 dollars,

20
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,900
each group must effectively make use
of their distinct advantages.

21
00:01:10,570 --> 00:01:11,570
You want us to collude?

22
00:01:11,570 --> 00:01:13,040
How much should we raise the food prices?

23
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:14,110
We're not going to sell them anyway.

24
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,080
They're going to starve everyone all day?

25
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:17,480
Or we'll turn currency
into worthless trash.

26
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:18,680
Hyperinflation.

27
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:20,880
Fear is driving everything.

28
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:22,120
I should cause pollution.

29
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:23,580
When we have the ultimate power
over their lives,

30
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:25,090
those who are backed into a corner
will bite.

31
00:01:25,290 --> 00:01:27,960
You must read trades and negotiations...

32
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:29,420
that satisfy everyone.

33
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:32,990
It's just so infuriating.
Seriously. I'm so angry.

34
00:01:33,090 --> 00:01:35,300
It's going just as I planned.

35
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:38,170
Complete coexistence is impossible
with the current number.

36
00:01:38,170 --> 00:01:39,600
Starve while keeping the peace.

37
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,070
Or start a war and shake things up.

38
00:01:41,070 --> 00:01:42,740
We have no choice but to start a war.

39
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:43,770
What's the purpose?

40
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:44,810
Cut down the number of mouths to feed.

41
00:01:44,970 --> 00:01:47,280
The moment a war breaks out,
there's no justice.

42
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:49,310
This is pure hypocrisy
from start to finish.

43
00:01:49,310 --> 00:01:51,910
So the leader can eliminate
members of the group.

44
00:01:51,910 --> 00:01:53,050
- Right.
- Yes.

45
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:57,420
Just because we had equal opportunities,
do you think our outcomes are fair?

46
00:01:57,490 --> 00:01:58,690
Starting a war means...

47
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:00,660
everyone will criticize you for it.

48
00:02:00,890 --> 00:02:03,930
This new world sounds amazing.
Do you want to check it out?

49
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:06,460
Before moving into Community Town,

50
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:09,000
all the residents took a test...

51
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:12,930
to assess their values and mindsets
toward financial decisions.

52
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:15,340
"A bomb threat was reported
at the Sports Complex..."

53
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:16,700
"where thousands of people are gathered."

54
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:18,940
"The only way to locate the bomb..."

55
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:22,010
"is through torturing
the terrorist's innocent child."

56
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:23,480
This is an intense question.

57
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,250
Is this about the trolley dilemma?

58
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:29,320
I might be able to torture the terrorist.

59
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:31,850
So it's utilitarianism versus Kantianism.

60
00:02:32,190 --> 00:02:34,020
My gosh. What an awful question.

61
00:02:34,090 --> 00:02:35,460
It's tiring me out.

62
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:39,060
"When a CEO earns hundreds of times
more than average workers,"

63
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:40,730
"it can hardly be justified
as fair compensation for performance."

64
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:43,060
Yes, it is fair compensation, everyone.

65
00:02:43,230 --> 00:02:44,870
Work hard and become a CEO.

66
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:49,500
"The main reason the poor stay poor is
a lack of effort and will."

67
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:51,370
My gosh, this is sad.

68
00:02:51,540 --> 00:02:53,340
This question is making me really sad.

69
00:02:53,570 --> 00:02:55,110
Do people still say this these days?

70
00:02:55,810 --> 00:02:58,410
There's an expression that goes,
"rags to riches."

71
00:02:58,410 --> 00:02:59,710
Even if you're dealt the worst hand,

72
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:02,480
as long as you desperately keep trying,
you can be successful.

73
00:03:02,550 --> 00:03:05,190
"In order to resolve
the national shortage of electricity,"

74
00:03:05,190 --> 00:03:06,850
"a dam must be built."

75
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:08,620
(In order to resolve
the national shortage of electricity,)

76
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:10,390
(a dam must be built...)

77
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:12,330
(even over the objections
of the residents in the flooded zone.)

78
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:13,830
I can't be a politician.

79
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:16,300
Politicians are no joke.

80
00:03:16,300 --> 00:03:18,670
Is the agenda of the show
making us reflect on ourselves?

81
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,970
My opinions are starting to change
as the questions go on.

82
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:25,810
I don't think you guys can go home today.

83
00:03:26,370 --> 00:03:28,110
The Community Town Test...

84
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:31,750
measures criteria for judgment
and decisions across three levels,

85
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:34,080
and they are ethics, society,
and the individual.

86
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:36,950
The white on the left represents
an idealistic mindset,

87
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,890
and the black on the right represents
a pragmatic mindset.

88
00:03:41,660 --> 00:03:44,260
The ethical level identifies
the decision-making criteria...

89
00:03:44,260 --> 00:03:46,090
when facing moral dilemmas.

90
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:48,460
Prioritizing established principles...

91
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:51,700
over individual limitations
aligns with principlism.

92
00:03:52,370 --> 00:03:54,470
Prioritizing to maximize...

93
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:58,140
the overall benefit even at the expense
of subjective decisions...

94
00:03:58,310 --> 00:04:00,110
aligns with consequentialism.

95
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:03,080
"Even if an analysis
of various records and circumstances..."

96
00:04:03,080 --> 00:04:05,480
"clearly predicts that
someone will commit a serious crime,"

97
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:07,420
"they cannot be detained..."

98
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:09,380
"until the actual crime..."

99
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:10,920
"has been committed." Of course, not.

100
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:12,950
What is this? A sci-fi movie?

101
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:15,890
A crime is proven through actions.

102
00:04:15,990 --> 00:04:18,030
But there's something called
crime prevention.

103
00:04:18,030 --> 00:04:21,530
In the US, terrorism task forces
continually investigate...

104
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:24,000
individuals with a high risk
of committing terrorism...

105
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,400
and try to apprehend them.

106
00:04:25,400 --> 00:04:28,370
I believe that for our society
to grow in a healthy way,

107
00:04:28,370 --> 00:04:31,470
doing this much is reasonable.
Come on, help us out here.

108
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:32,970
There's a 99.9 percent chance
that they will commit crimes.

109
00:04:33,270 --> 00:04:36,010
"A company that violates
major public interest..."

110
00:04:36,010 --> 00:04:39,780
"must be reported even if doing so
causes many innocent people..."

111
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:40,880
"to lose their jobs."

112
00:04:41,220 --> 00:04:43,920
Does protecting
the major public interest...

113
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:45,590
truly outweigh the loss...

114
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:46,990
of innocent people's jobs?

115
00:04:47,120 --> 00:04:48,360
I want to weigh the importance.

116
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,260
For example, what if
only 100 people were harmed by it?

117
00:04:50,260 --> 00:04:52,960
If the numbers are about the same,
they might weigh the same.

118
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:54,200
I won't report it.

119
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:56,900
If they end up losing their jobs,
whose interest is actually being served?

120
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,700
But a lot of people will lose their jobs.

121
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:01,770
How can I report the company? I can't.

122
00:05:01,770 --> 00:05:04,470
But the implied context
in this sentence...

123
00:05:04,470 --> 00:05:06,040
is really infuriating.

124
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:07,640
I think the company should be reported.

125
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:10,510
Did I just put a bunch of people
out of work?

126
00:05:10,810 --> 00:05:12,710
Yes, people will lose their jobs.

127
00:05:12,710 --> 00:05:16,480
But the societal wealth
could be redirected elsewhere.

128
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:19,890
A company that violates
major public interest must be reported.

129
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:22,620
This is too hard. But I should do it.

130
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:26,760
The societal level identifies
the worldview about the distribution...

131
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,230
of economic resources in society.

132
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:31,370
Prioritizing institutions and policies...

133
00:05:31,370 --> 00:05:33,700
to reduce inequality
is classified as structuralism.

134
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:37,440
Prioritizing individual effort
and talent...

135
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:41,380
over systemic factors
is classified as meritocracy.

136
00:05:41,810 --> 00:05:43,280
"Even when hiring for organizations..."

137
00:05:43,280 --> 00:05:44,780
"or corporations was purely based
on merit,"

138
00:05:44,780 --> 00:05:47,520
"adjustments should be made
if diversity falls..."

139
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:48,850
"severely out of balance."

140
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:51,890
No. Merit comes first.

141
00:05:51,890 --> 00:05:52,950
It's a business, after all.

142
00:05:52,950 --> 00:05:54,560
What? What does this mean?

143
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:55,920
I guess it just happened naturally.

144
00:05:55,990 --> 00:05:57,120
It doesn't need to be adjusted.

145
00:05:57,220 --> 00:05:59,190
What I hate the most is
reverse discrimination.

146
00:05:59,290 --> 00:06:03,130
I think various factors
must be taken into account.

147
00:06:03,230 --> 00:06:04,700
Adjustments should be made.

148
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:06,400
That's better for companies too.

149
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,940
When like-minded people gather,
they only reinforce similar ideas.

150
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:11,010
Synergy is stronger
when diverse people come together.

151
00:06:11,010 --> 00:06:13,170
A lot of companies have adopted
this approach these days.

152
00:06:13,170 --> 00:06:16,410
Many female journalists
get hired these days.

153
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,980
If I'm working in HR at a company,

154
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:22,680
I'd think diversity yields better results.
According to my firsthand experience,

155
00:06:22,680 --> 00:06:25,220
I'd think that this approach is
more likely to bring...

156
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:26,850
better results.

157
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:30,420
"The decline of regional cities is
a natural outcome of market dynamics."

158
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:31,690
"Therefore, spending excessive budgets
to prevent it..."

159
00:06:31,690 --> 00:06:32,960
"is a waste of national resources."

160
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:36,130
No way.
Regional cities must be revitalized.

161
00:06:36,130 --> 00:06:37,600
This is especially the case in Korea.

162
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:38,970
Everyone comes to Seoul.

163
00:06:38,970 --> 00:06:42,940
But in the long view, regional cities
have to grow for our country to thrive.

164
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:44,710
We must spend excessive budgets on them.

165
00:06:44,710 --> 00:06:47,040
There are more tasks
our government has to handle now.

166
00:06:47,210 --> 00:06:50,180
Is it possible for the government
to prevent the decline of these cities?

167
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:53,310
The government grants subsidies
to farming and fishing villages.

168
00:06:53,550 --> 00:06:57,890
So I read in an article that
some people did move to those villages.

169
00:06:58,150 --> 00:07:01,960
But I don't think it's something
sustainable in the long run.

170
00:07:01,960 --> 00:07:03,990
People love Seoul way too much.

171
00:07:03,990 --> 00:07:05,990
We need a different approach.

172
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:08,630
So it might be
a waste of national resources.

173
00:07:09,230 --> 00:07:12,830
The individual level identifies
an individual's life priorities.

174
00:07:13,030 --> 00:07:14,940
Placing greater value
on non-monetary aspects...

175
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:17,470
is classified as leaning toward meaning.

176
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:22,010
Emphasizing that economic stability
underpins all other values...

177
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:24,040
is classified as leaning toward utility.

178
00:07:24,180 --> 00:07:25,850
"Even if a politician is
somewhat corrupt,"

179
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:28,220
"I'd vote for them
as long as they grow my wealth."

180
00:07:28,220 --> 00:07:30,050
Gosh,
a lot of people already vote like this.

181
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:32,150
It's like this everywhere, not just here.

182
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:36,020
People vote
in order to grow their interest.

183
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:38,530
If corruption is tolerable,

184
00:07:38,530 --> 00:07:40,590
some people may vote for them.

185
00:07:40,800 --> 00:07:42,060
How much are we talking?

186
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,270
It's not against the law.

187
00:07:44,270 --> 00:07:46,430
Right. I won't be violating the law.

188
00:07:46,430 --> 00:07:48,300
I'd justify my action with this excuse
and vote for them...

189
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:49,640
while I enjoy watching my wealth grow.

190
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:51,040
I can never do that.

191
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:54,780
I don't have that much money, anyway.
Grow my wealth?

192
00:07:55,010 --> 00:07:56,180
I won't vote for them.

193
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:57,610
How corrupt are we talking?

194
00:07:58,050 --> 00:08:00,010
I know that this politician is corrupt.

195
00:08:00,010 --> 00:08:02,020
But despite that, I might vote for them.
Right?

196
00:08:02,020 --> 00:08:03,880
I can't vote for them
if I know about corruption.

197
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:07,120
"When choosing a career,
personal aptitude and fulfillment..."

198
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:09,220
"must take priority
over bank account figures."

199
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:10,420
Personally speaking,

200
00:08:10,420 --> 00:08:12,990
I considered my aptitude
and fulfillment in choosing my career.

201
00:08:13,490 --> 00:08:14,900
Jobs are all about bank account figures.

202
00:08:14,900 --> 00:08:18,400
In my opinion,
people should work for monetary gains.

203
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,470
Aptitude and fulfillment are for hobbies.

204
00:08:20,670 --> 00:08:22,740
For example, I could get paid
100,000 dollars every day,

205
00:08:22,740 --> 00:08:25,810
but I get so depressed and am miserable
whenever I have to work.

206
00:08:26,110 --> 00:08:27,310
Then does the money mean anything?

207
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:31,310
The symbols representing values
across the three levels...

208
00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:34,950
are divided into two.
High-density symbols indicate...

209
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:36,980
strong tendencies...

210
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:39,850
and low-density symbols indicate
weak tendencies.

211
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:43,960
When faced with limited resources
and conflicting interests,

212
00:08:44,390 --> 00:08:46,760
what choices will the residents...

213
00:08:47,090 --> 00:08:49,030
with different sets of values make?

214
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:53,130
Will they be able to reach negotiations...

215
00:08:53,130 --> 00:08:55,240
and the perfect balance...

216
00:08:55,600 --> 00:08:57,770
everyone's satisfied with?

217
00:08:59,510 --> 00:09:01,510
All right then.

218
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:04,480
The game of trade and choice
will begin now.

219
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:08,120
(The Community 2: Invisible Hand)

220
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:13,920
(1: What I Have and What You Want)

221
00:09:23,130 --> 00:09:25,730
Hello. My name is Park Se Seung.

222
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:29,470
I actually started out
as a corporate strategy consultant.

223
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:33,510
After I starred on a dating show,

224
00:09:33,510 --> 00:09:36,810
I started working full-time as a YouTuber.

225
00:09:36,810 --> 00:09:39,010
Of course,
I'm going to survive until the end.

226
00:09:39,010 --> 00:09:41,520
If we're playing games,
I have to win first place no matter what.

227
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:43,920
There it is.

228
00:09:45,390 --> 00:09:47,890
(Community Center)

229
00:09:55,800 --> 00:09:56,830
My gosh.

230
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,500
(Community Center)

231
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:05,140
It looks much cooler than before.

232
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:10,840
Luna, welcome to Community Town.

233
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:14,580
Please collect your name tag
and resident notebook.

234
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:23,590
My nickname is Luna.

235
00:10:23,790 --> 00:10:25,730
Moonlight is subtle, isn't it?

236
00:10:25,860 --> 00:10:28,100
It softly reaches everyone.

237
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:30,430
I want to be someone
who influences others like...

238
00:10:30,430 --> 00:10:33,370
how moonlight touches them.
So I picked Luna.

239
00:10:33,630 --> 00:10:36,400
Luna, your color is white.

240
00:10:36,800 --> 00:10:40,410
Please check your values code too.

241
00:10:40,840 --> 00:10:42,140
I'd suggest...

242
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:45,110
keeping your values code a secret
from the other residents.

243
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:46,680
(Practicality: 1, Ability: 2,
Principle: 1)

244
00:10:46,680 --> 00:10:49,850
(She's realistic
and values individual ability.)

245
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:51,920
When I watch competition shows...

246
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:54,920
Gosh, if I say this,
I might sound quite cruel.

247
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:57,390
Contestants
who deserve to be eliminated...

248
00:10:57,660 --> 00:10:59,890
It depends on everyone's ability.

249
00:10:59,890 --> 00:11:01,960
On competition shows,
it's all about the games.

250
00:11:01,960 --> 00:11:04,370
Contestants who are better at these games
will survive longer.

251
00:11:04,370 --> 00:11:06,930
And those contestants
should take bigger prize money.

252
00:11:06,930 --> 00:11:09,100
That's the rule of these shows.

253
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:12,370
I'm quite thoughtful
and considerate of others.

254
00:11:12,370 --> 00:11:13,910
But despite that, I put myself first.

255
00:11:13,910 --> 00:11:16,180
I look after others
after securing my survival.

256
00:11:16,180 --> 00:11:17,280
When planes are crashing,

257
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:19,280
you have to put on your oxygen mask first
before helping others.

258
00:11:19,410 --> 00:11:20,710
That's how I'd describe it.

259
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:22,980
Let's say that I put on my oxygen mask.

260
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:24,950
Would I remove the oxygen mask
from the person next to me?

261
00:11:24,950 --> 00:11:26,750
I hope I don't do that.

262
00:11:27,050 --> 00:11:28,290
I don't think I'd do that.

263
00:11:30,190 --> 00:11:32,090
Resident registration is complete.

264
00:11:32,430 --> 00:11:34,460
Please go to your dormitory.

265
00:11:34,460 --> 00:11:35,530
Here I go.

266
00:11:36,560 --> 00:11:37,630
White...

267
00:11:38,570 --> 00:11:39,970
White. Okay.

268
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:54,850
(Community Center, Staircase, White)

269
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:07,060
I made it.

270
00:12:08,230 --> 00:12:12,730
(Dormitory for White)

271
00:12:18,210 --> 00:12:19,570
Nice to meet you.

272
00:12:19,570 --> 00:12:22,410
I'm running an organization
called Feminism with Him.

273
00:12:22,610 --> 00:12:25,380
I earn my living as an educator
in sex education, gender equality,

274
00:12:25,380 --> 00:12:27,010
and violence prevention.

275
00:12:27,110 --> 00:12:29,450
I'm a gender equality activist.
My name is Lee Han.

276
00:12:29,450 --> 00:12:30,980
When people hear that I'm an educator...

277
00:12:30,980 --> 00:12:33,550
for sex education and violence prevention,

278
00:12:33,750 --> 00:12:36,390
a lot of them start off
by crossing their arms.

279
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:37,960
And of course, they display hostility...

280
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:39,960
because they think I'm treating them
as potential perpetrators.

281
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:41,600
To be honest, I like that.

282
00:12:41,960 --> 00:12:43,630
Let's say I go to an all-boys' high school
and the first question they ask me is,

283
00:12:43,630 --> 00:12:45,200
"What do you think about prostitution?"

284
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:47,840
Then I think, "This will be a fun class"
as I walk in.

285
00:12:47,970 --> 00:12:51,070
Up until a few years ago,
I wasn't aware of this problem.

286
00:12:51,170 --> 00:12:55,010
When I incorporate my experience
into my lectures,

287
00:12:55,010 --> 00:12:57,210
I get a lot more comments like,

288
00:12:57,210 --> 00:12:59,250
"I've never heard of this before."

289
00:12:59,410 --> 00:13:01,480
On this show, we're basically locked up
and forced to talk.

290
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:04,050
You guys couldn't have given me
a better environment than this.

291
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:12,260
Siseon, welcome to Community Town.

292
00:13:12,460 --> 00:13:13,560
Thank you.

293
00:13:14,390 --> 00:13:16,760
(He spots the resident notebook
on the table.)

294
00:13:16,830 --> 00:13:18,930
(Nickname: Siseon, Group: White)

295
00:13:20,230 --> 00:13:21,900
(He has a strong
and consistent idealistic tendency.)

296
00:13:21,900 --> 00:13:23,570
That's easy. Good. That's a relief.

297
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:26,570
My nickname is Siseon.

298
00:13:26,840 --> 00:13:29,180
I actually got the nickname
from one of my favorite novels,

299
00:13:29,180 --> 00:13:31,110
"From the Gaze" by Chung Se Rang.

300
00:13:31,180 --> 00:13:34,180
The female characters in the novel
are quite cool.

301
00:13:34,310 --> 00:13:38,690
It's important and necessary
for me to have various perspectives.

302
00:13:38,690 --> 00:13:41,190
I thought this nickname suited my career.

303
00:13:41,190 --> 00:13:42,920
That's why I decided on Siseon
as my nickname.

304
00:13:47,030 --> 00:13:49,600
Hello. My name is Lee Hyun Seon.

305
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:53,270
I work for Explosive Ordnance Disposal
at Korea Airports Corporation.

306
00:13:53,430 --> 00:13:55,170
Ever since I was in fourth grade,

307
00:13:55,170 --> 00:13:57,940
I just wanted to join the military.

308
00:13:57,940 --> 00:14:01,240
When I was in the special forces,
I served as a demolition specialist.

309
00:14:01,410 --> 00:14:02,880
After getting discharged,

310
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:06,110
I wanted to continue the career path
I began in the military.

311
00:14:06,350 --> 00:14:08,220
So I decided to apply for KAC.

312
00:14:12,450 --> 00:14:17,220
(Nickname: Boss, Group: White)

313
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:20,860
I wanted to go with Big Boss like
the character Song Joong Ki played...

314
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:22,430
in "Descendants of the Sun."

315
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:24,870
I asked my family
what they thought of my nickname.

316
00:14:24,970 --> 00:14:28,370
They said "big" didn't suit me.
So I just went with Boss.

317
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:32,440
"Meaning, Ability, and Principle."

318
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:33,570
(She's an idealist
but values individual ability.)

319
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:34,980
I value...

320
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:38,210
people and honor over money.

321
00:14:38,210 --> 00:14:41,180
It made me want to survive on this show...

322
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:43,580
without abandoning my values.

323
00:14:47,350 --> 00:14:48,890
My name is KNUCKS.

324
00:14:49,160 --> 00:14:50,560
I'm a professional dancer.

325
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:54,390
Every competition show I've been on
has always been an absolute battlefield.

326
00:14:54,660 --> 00:14:58,430
As for "Bloody Game,"
it was a pretty intense game.

327
00:14:58,570 --> 00:15:01,100
"Street Man Fighter" was also intense.

328
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:03,400
When I watched the first season
of The Community,

329
00:15:03,570 --> 00:15:05,270
the show was quite thought-provoking.

330
00:15:05,370 --> 00:15:06,810
I may not look like the type,

331
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:10,380
but I'm quite gentle.

332
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:12,350
I thought I would learn a lot
from this show.

333
00:15:12,350 --> 00:15:14,010
So I'm looking forward to my time here.

334
00:15:15,080 --> 00:15:16,080
Goodness.

335
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:20,050
Paul, welcome to Community Town.

336
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:24,320
Please collect your name tag
and resident notebook.

337
00:15:24,320 --> 00:15:25,390
My gosh.

338
00:15:27,630 --> 00:15:28,700
This is pretty cool.

339
00:15:28,700 --> 00:15:30,030
(Nickname: Paul, Group: White)

340
00:15:30,030 --> 00:15:32,930
"Paul" is actually a religious name.

341
00:15:32,930 --> 00:15:34,400
Here's how I'd describe him.

342
00:15:34,400 --> 00:15:38,140
Through an unexpected event,
he broke out of his existing views...

343
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:39,740
and spared his faith.

344
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:42,310
To be honest, if there are things
I need to change about myself,

345
00:15:42,310 --> 00:15:45,110
I'm ready to change them
to turn the tables around on this show.

346
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:46,650
So I went with Paul.

347
00:15:48,220 --> 00:15:49,480
"Ability, one."

348
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:51,890
(Meaning: 2, Ability: 1, Principle: 2)

349
00:15:51,890 --> 00:15:53,650
This isn't my ability level.

350
00:15:53,650 --> 00:15:55,090
(He's a strict idealist
who values individual ability.)

351
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:02,460
(The veteran contestant
looks around the center.)

352
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:03,900
I see something here.

353
00:16:04,460 --> 00:16:05,470
Okay.

354
00:16:06,570 --> 00:16:07,630
Something's here.

355
00:16:10,370 --> 00:16:11,370
It's cool.

356
00:16:17,340 --> 00:16:18,710
My name is Shin Hye Won.

357
00:16:19,010 --> 00:16:21,350
I've been a journalist for 14 years.

358
00:16:21,720 --> 00:16:24,280
I mostly covered
social issues and politics.

359
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:25,890
Back when I was
covering the National Assembly,

360
00:16:25,890 --> 00:16:27,050
I alternated between...

361
00:16:27,050 --> 00:16:29,120
covering the ruling and opposition parties
for a long time.

362
00:16:29,260 --> 00:16:34,030
I meet real-life politicians who operate
in the world of actual politics.

363
00:16:34,290 --> 00:16:36,400
Despite the political figures...

364
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:40,130
participating in the first season,

365
00:16:40,300 --> 00:16:43,040
it was very much idealistic
compared to actual politics.

366
00:16:43,070 --> 00:16:44,670
In the first season,

367
00:16:44,670 --> 00:16:48,580
individual judgment regarding values
was the key element.

368
00:16:48,580 --> 00:16:51,510
But these elected politicians...

369
00:16:51,710 --> 00:16:54,580
don't act purely out of their values.

370
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,190
I believe that it happens quite often
that they champion certain values...

371
00:16:58,190 --> 00:16:59,750
only for those values
to turn out to be self-serving.

372
00:17:00,020 --> 00:17:01,990
Yes. I have to be careful.

373
00:17:04,260 --> 00:17:06,190
(Nickname: Leia, Group: White)

374
00:17:08,960 --> 00:17:10,300
(Leia)

375
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:12,900
(Meaning: 2, Structure: 1, Principle: 2)

376
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:16,040
(Her tendency is consistently idealistic.)

377
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:20,740
I gave myself a nickname
after a character in "Star Wars."

378
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:23,580
She's a princess,
but doesn't shy away from the conflicts.

379
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:25,910
She takes charge at the forefront.

380
00:17:25,910 --> 00:17:28,150
After watching this side of her character
in the movie,

381
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:30,550
I picked her name to be my nickname.

382
00:17:35,290 --> 00:17:38,160
I have a unique job.
I'm a board game developer.

383
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:39,330
My name is Kim Geon Hee.

384
00:17:39,330 --> 00:17:42,000
You know games
like Halli Galli, Zenga, and Rummikub?

385
00:17:42,130 --> 00:17:43,930
Every board game
has a developer behind it.

386
00:17:44,160 --> 00:17:45,770
It's like how books have authors.

387
00:17:45,770 --> 00:17:47,900
Board game developers come up with rules.

388
00:17:48,100 --> 00:17:51,140
People follow the rules
while playing the games,

389
00:17:51,140 --> 00:17:52,370
and they have fun.

390
00:17:52,370 --> 00:17:55,910
My job is to design
that kind of fun through games.

391
00:17:56,810 --> 00:17:59,410
"Resident notebook of Community Town."
"Hamlet."

392
00:17:59,410 --> 00:18:02,080
(Nickname: Hamlet, Group: White)

393
00:18:02,080 --> 00:18:03,750
My nickname is Hamlet.

394
00:18:03,750 --> 00:18:06,520
I actually think
Hamlet is a strong character.

395
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,020
When you read "Hamlet,"
it seems everyone has a plan.

396
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:09,560
Some plot revenge and so on.

397
00:18:09,860 --> 00:18:12,190
They try to be smart and make plans.
But they all end up dead.

398
00:18:12,330 --> 00:18:15,500
It's all about his survival.
He has to consider death.

399
00:18:15,700 --> 00:18:18,800
Just like him, I'll put my life
on the line and try my best to survive.

400
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:20,570
(Meaning: 2, Structure: 2, Principle: 2)

401
00:18:20,570 --> 00:18:22,300
(Hamlet is a strict idealist.)

402
00:18:22,570 --> 00:18:23,800
I was surprised.

403
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:25,340
"Was I this transparent?"

404
00:18:25,540 --> 00:18:28,040
Here's an example.
It was easy for me to decide...

405
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:30,810
when I was forced to violate existing laws
to do something.

406
00:18:30,910 --> 00:18:33,950
A lot of people must have spent years...

407
00:18:33,950 --> 00:18:36,180
deliberating to craft these laws.

408
00:18:36,550 --> 00:18:39,750
But should I violate these laws
to pursue what I believe is justice?

409
00:18:39,750 --> 00:18:41,660
I shouldn't do that.
I clearly knew where I stood there.

410
00:18:44,390 --> 00:18:46,390
"Resident notebook of Community Town."

411
00:19:01,310 --> 00:19:02,710
"Resource storage."

412
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:11,080
- Hello.
- My gosh, hello.

413
00:19:11,380 --> 00:19:13,190
I should have opened the door for you.

414
00:19:13,450 --> 00:19:15,620
Boss? Our boss is here.

415
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:17,860
- Everyone is in White?
- Yes, we're all in White.

416
00:19:17,860 --> 00:19:19,390
I think this is the dormitory for White.

417
00:19:19,390 --> 00:19:20,460
The bathroom.

418
00:19:20,930 --> 00:19:22,600
You have to push it
if you want to open it.

419
00:19:22,600 --> 00:19:24,000
- Hello.
- Nice to meet you.

420
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,230
This can't be everyone.

421
00:19:25,230 --> 00:19:27,330
- This is just for White.
- This is for Group White.

422
00:19:27,330 --> 00:19:28,900
- I guess this is a team match.
- Yes.

423
00:19:28,970 --> 00:19:31,140
You must've been flustered
since you were the first one in.

424
00:19:31,270 --> 00:19:32,910
- There are three rooms.
- Twenty people...

425
00:19:33,070 --> 00:19:34,370
- That's...
- All the rooms look great.

426
00:19:34,670 --> 00:19:36,280
- So 3 of us might have to share it.
- There are only 3 rooms.

427
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:38,380
The three of us might end up
sharing the room together.

428
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:39,380
That's possible.

429
00:19:40,710 --> 00:19:44,380
I think the staff picked
only good-looking people.

430
00:19:45,290 --> 00:19:46,450
Everyone here is so young.

431
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:51,290
Having a chance to communicate
with young people is quite rare...

432
00:19:51,290 --> 00:19:52,430
- these days.
- So you don't consider yourself young?

433
00:19:52,430 --> 00:19:54,190
I wouldn't say I'm young.

434
00:19:54,190 --> 00:19:55,660
- I'm not young.
- Gosh, you are young.

435
00:19:55,660 --> 00:19:56,660
No.

436
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:58,700
I watched the first season.

437
00:19:59,330 --> 00:20:00,930
- It's very different.
- Yes.

438
00:20:00,930 --> 00:20:03,100
- This is a completely different concept.
- Yes.

439
00:20:03,170 --> 00:20:04,570
- But...
- No one can see this, right?

440
00:20:04,570 --> 00:20:05,570
- Right.
- Right.

441
00:20:05,570 --> 00:20:07,540
If you think about it, we'll be roommates.

442
00:20:07,640 --> 00:20:10,340
Trying to figure out
each other's values code at night...

443
00:20:10,340 --> 00:20:12,280
might be a bit hard.

444
00:20:12,550 --> 00:20:14,780
In the last season of The Community,

445
00:20:15,350 --> 00:20:17,020
the test ranked the residents...

446
00:20:17,350 --> 00:20:20,920
across 4 levels with 3 sub-tiers each.

447
00:20:21,620 --> 00:20:22,790
(Ideology Verification Open)

448
00:20:22,790 --> 00:20:23,790
There was a rule...

449
00:20:23,790 --> 00:20:26,030
where guessing another resident's score
by using this information...

450
00:20:26,290 --> 00:20:28,130
would get them eliminated.

451
00:20:28,630 --> 00:20:31,160
Attacking another resident
with Ideology Verification was possible.

452
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:32,230
(Ideology Verification Attack
utilized the scores.)

453
00:20:32,330 --> 00:20:35,140
So the code has three letters.
And the number represents...

454
00:20:35,540 --> 00:20:36,800
the level of intensity, right?

455
00:20:36,940 --> 00:20:39,270
- One must be moderate. Two, intense.
- Two.

456
00:20:39,270 --> 00:20:40,410
- That must be it.
- That's it.

457
00:20:40,670 --> 00:20:42,780
Then isn't it much easier
to figure out the code?

458
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:44,310
- It's much easier compared to before.
- Yes.

459
00:20:44,310 --> 00:20:45,950
There are fewer combinations now.

460
00:20:45,950 --> 00:20:47,510
As long as we don't attack each other...

461
00:20:48,380 --> 00:20:50,550
- I'm in big trouble.
- You know what I just thought of?

462
00:20:50,780 --> 00:20:54,850
I'm getting a feeling that
most of us might be leaning toward White.

463
00:20:55,090 --> 00:20:56,090
What?

464
00:20:56,260 --> 00:20:57,990
- That crossed my mind.
- You gave us a hint.

465
00:20:57,990 --> 00:20:59,230
- But...
- Well...

466
00:21:00,730 --> 00:21:02,300
What you just said
could have caused a lot of trouble.

467
00:21:02,300 --> 00:21:04,430
- He gave us a hint.
- That's how much I trust you guys.

468
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:05,800
- Already?
- Right?

469
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:07,630
- Yes. I don't want to fight either.
- Yes.

470
00:21:07,830 --> 00:21:10,600
- I already like you guys.
- That was sudden.

471
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,470
- One of us could be leaning toward Black.
- That's possible.

472
00:21:13,640 --> 00:21:15,980
It's possible that someone has all Blacks.

473
00:21:17,310 --> 00:21:18,950
- Let's be careful.
- Be careful.

474
00:21:19,510 --> 00:21:20,550
I'll do my best.

475
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:22,880
- I get why we ended up in Group White.
- Why?

476
00:21:22,880 --> 00:21:26,350
Everyone looks so innocent,
like the color white.

477
00:21:27,150 --> 00:21:28,190
All of you.

478
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:32,390
I guess it's Black and White.

479
00:21:32,390 --> 00:21:33,890
- That might be the theme, right?
- Right?

480
00:21:36,900 --> 00:21:38,030
- We're Red.
- Yes.

481
00:21:38,570 --> 00:21:40,530
I think we're split
into color-coded teams.

482
00:21:41,700 --> 00:21:44,470
- Do you think we're in here together...
- Right. We...

483
00:21:44,470 --> 00:21:45,870
because we have things in common?

484
00:21:46,440 --> 00:21:47,610
- Well, should we...
- You like red.

485
00:21:47,670 --> 00:21:49,680
open up about our political leanings?

486
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:52,450
- Why red?
- Right.

487
00:21:54,250 --> 00:21:55,520
Hello.

488
00:21:55,650 --> 00:21:57,820
I'm participating as Donkey.

489
00:21:57,820 --> 00:21:59,620
I'm a stand-up comedian, Park Cheol Hyeon.

490
00:22:00,020 --> 00:22:01,690
I chose Donkey as my nickname...

491
00:22:01,890 --> 00:22:04,420
because I actually like donkeys.

492
00:22:04,560 --> 00:22:08,190
Horses jump off a cliff
when their owners tell them to jump.

493
00:22:08,260 --> 00:22:10,900
But donkeys go,
"What? If I jump, I'll die."

494
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:12,630
They can be stubborn like that.

495
00:22:12,730 --> 00:22:15,470
Let's say...
Do you call them reins? Or a leash?

496
00:22:15,470 --> 00:22:18,510
Anyway, even if someone's holding them,
I want to be someone...

497
00:22:18,610 --> 00:22:20,940
who's stubborn enough
to make his own decisions and speak up.

498
00:22:20,940 --> 00:22:22,310
So I chose Donkey.

499
00:22:22,640 --> 00:22:24,740
(He values meaning over money.)

500
00:22:24,740 --> 00:22:27,780
(But he's a strict realist.)

501
00:22:27,850 --> 00:22:33,290
I remember a lot of questions
similar to the trolley dilemma.

502
00:22:33,390 --> 00:22:35,360
My thoughts depended on the day.

503
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:37,690
On days I was stressed,

504
00:22:37,690 --> 00:22:39,730
I felt like wishing a lot of them dead.

505
00:22:41,360 --> 00:22:42,700
You know what it's like.

506
00:22:42,730 --> 00:22:45,770
Even if I act in good conscience,

507
00:22:45,770 --> 00:22:49,900
there are instances
where many of those involved get hurt.

508
00:22:49,900 --> 00:22:52,510
When I feel like problems...

509
00:22:52,510 --> 00:22:54,040
are beyond my control,

510
00:22:54,040 --> 00:22:56,080
I tend to pull back from them.

511
00:22:56,680 --> 00:22:57,910
I'm Donkey.

512
00:22:57,910 --> 00:22:58,950
- Donkey?
- Yes.

513
00:22:58,950 --> 00:23:00,550
You can call me Izaha.

514
00:23:00,550 --> 00:23:01,750
- Izaha?
- Yes.

515
00:23:01,750 --> 00:23:03,550
- I have a four-syllable name.
- Right.

516
00:23:03,550 --> 00:23:05,250
- Maillard.
- You can call me however you want.

517
00:23:05,250 --> 00:23:06,590
I'm guessing
you're pretty good at cooking.

518
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:10,190
I swear...

519
00:23:10,190 --> 00:23:11,320
- I've seen you around.
- Right.

520
00:23:11,790 --> 00:23:12,830
I don't know who you are exactly,

521
00:23:12,830 --> 00:23:14,530
- but you seem like a good cook.
- Right.

522
00:23:16,100 --> 00:23:17,530
My name is Mok Jin Hwa.

523
00:23:17,860 --> 00:23:19,570
I go by the handle "Seungwoos Dad,"

524
00:23:19,570 --> 00:23:22,070
and I post cooking videos.

525
00:23:22,800 --> 00:23:24,440
My nickname is Maillard,

526
00:23:24,440 --> 00:23:27,270
which comes from what made me famous...

527
00:23:27,270 --> 00:23:29,110
in my videos.

528
00:23:29,180 --> 00:23:30,680
The Maillard reaction...

529
00:23:30,680 --> 00:23:33,580
refers to the browning process
that happens...

530
00:23:33,580 --> 00:23:36,180
when meat is cooked at high heat.

531
00:23:36,950 --> 00:23:38,420
A lot of the questions...

532
00:23:38,420 --> 00:23:41,120
were about
whether I'd sacrifice the minority...

533
00:23:41,190 --> 00:23:42,920
for the greater good.

534
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:44,990
I'd willingly sacrifice myself instead.

535
00:23:45,060 --> 00:23:46,430
I play online games,

536
00:23:46,430 --> 00:23:47,530
and moments come up...

537
00:23:47,530 --> 00:23:49,530
where I have to choose
between killing 1 person or saving 5.

538
00:23:49,530 --> 00:23:51,060
I made that choice recently...

539
00:23:51,060 --> 00:23:52,870
to achieve our shared goal.

540
00:23:53,330 --> 00:23:54,600
Anyway, I'm a "T."

541
00:23:54,670 --> 00:23:58,570
I find it difficult to understand people
who always react...

542
00:23:58,570 --> 00:24:00,510
emotionally first.

543
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:01,910
Sleeping well...

544
00:24:02,410 --> 00:24:04,240
Wait, what are those numbers?

545
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:08,720
Is that a puzzle we need to solve?

546
00:24:08,820 --> 00:24:10,180
That's 352461...

547
00:24:10,750 --> 00:24:12,750
Don't you think there's something to it?

548
00:24:13,350 --> 00:24:15,560
There's a reason
they put books around here.

549
00:24:15,820 --> 00:24:17,590
(They take a strategic approach
from the very start.)

550
00:24:17,590 --> 00:24:19,330
The subtitle for the show
is "Invisible Hand."

551
00:24:19,330 --> 00:24:20,460
- I see.
- That's right.

552
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:24,330
It's the metaphor Adam Smith used
to illustrate free market principles.

553
00:24:24,330 --> 00:24:26,000
The economy must be the theme this time.

554
00:24:26,030 --> 00:24:27,200
This is about currency.

555
00:24:27,230 --> 00:24:30,800
It keeps going on about the trust
in the system.

556
00:24:30,870 --> 00:24:33,410
Maybe it's a contest to see
who can earn trust.

557
00:24:33,410 --> 00:24:35,580
Trust, the system...

558
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:37,140
I have a feeling
we'll be dabbling in that.

559
00:24:37,710 --> 00:24:39,850
Hi, I'm Lee Jae Yong.

560
00:24:39,850 --> 00:24:42,180
As an accountant,
I consult for small companies,

561
00:24:42,180 --> 00:24:43,550
make investments, give lectures,

562
00:24:43,550 --> 00:24:45,590
and make broadcast appearances,
among other things.

563
00:24:45,920 --> 00:24:47,320
In our field, accountants...

564
00:24:47,320 --> 00:24:49,620
are known as the watchdogs of capitalism.

565
00:24:49,620 --> 00:24:53,190
We analyze the situation
and offer advice...

566
00:24:53,190 --> 00:24:55,090
based on data...

567
00:24:55,090 --> 00:24:57,100
so others can make informed decisions.

568
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:58,430
(Izaha)

569
00:24:58,430 --> 00:24:59,900
I go by Izaha,

570
00:24:59,900 --> 00:25:03,470
named after the main character
in one of my favorite web novels,

571
00:25:03,670 --> 00:25:05,470
"Return of the Mad Demon."

572
00:25:05,570 --> 00:25:07,870
His nickname is actually Mad Demon,

573
00:25:07,870 --> 00:25:09,480
just like the title suggests,

574
00:25:09,480 --> 00:25:10,940
and his mood swings
are totally unpredictable.

575
00:25:10,940 --> 00:25:12,810
He can be ruthless one moment...

576
00:25:12,810 --> 00:25:14,510
and merciful the next,

577
00:25:14,510 --> 00:25:16,250
but he's genuinely considerate at heart.

578
00:25:16,250 --> 00:25:18,220
Deep down, he has a strong desire...

579
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:20,650
to protect the vulnerable and the weak.

580
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:23,260
I wanted to come across as fun,

581
00:25:23,260 --> 00:25:25,530
slightly unhinged, yet warmhearted,

582
00:25:25,560 --> 00:25:26,990
so that's why I chose that nickname.

583
00:25:26,990 --> 00:25:28,190
(Meaning 2, Structure 2, Principle 1)

584
00:25:28,360 --> 00:25:31,700
I feel a lot more at ease
knowing that we're a team.

585
00:25:31,700 --> 00:25:34,730
Here's one thing I was concerned about
before coming here.

586
00:25:34,730 --> 00:25:36,570
I was facing a real dilemma,

587
00:25:36,570 --> 00:25:38,970
knowing I'd be branded an enemy
the moment I even considered...

588
00:25:38,970 --> 00:25:40,940
- taking someone out.
- That's right.

589
00:25:40,940 --> 00:25:43,240
Realizing we're a team
made me see that we'll have...

590
00:25:43,240 --> 00:25:44,810
- to pull together to survive.
- Exactly.

591
00:25:44,810 --> 00:25:46,650
The moment you start plotting,

592
00:25:46,880 --> 00:25:48,050
you put a target on your back.

593
00:25:48,050 --> 00:25:49,320
I think that's why we were put...

594
00:25:49,320 --> 00:25:50,580
- in separate rooms.
- I agree.

595
00:25:52,050 --> 00:25:53,650
What happens...

596
00:25:53,890 --> 00:25:56,190
if we get a clean shot...

597
00:25:56,190 --> 00:25:58,190
- at taking out the other teams?
- Right.

598
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:01,430
Would you take them out
without hesitation?

599
00:26:06,470 --> 00:26:08,470
In that case,
I'm not planning on making nice...

600
00:26:08,470 --> 00:26:09,940
with the opposing teams.

601
00:26:11,600 --> 00:26:12,710
Of course.

602
00:26:14,070 --> 00:26:17,440
What do you think was the reason behind
giving us blue?

603
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:18,680
I'm drawing a blank.

604
00:26:18,880 --> 00:26:20,280
Are we competing as teams? No, right?

605
00:26:20,280 --> 00:26:21,780
- Is it a team game?
- Probably not, right?

606
00:26:22,580 --> 00:26:25,020
You look awfully familiar.

607
00:26:25,020 --> 00:26:26,090
- Really?
- Yes.

608
00:26:26,090 --> 00:26:27,350
You might've seen me before.

609
00:26:27,850 --> 00:26:29,290
I could be mistaken, but...

610
00:26:30,890 --> 00:26:32,490
That could be the case.

611
00:26:33,390 --> 00:26:35,300
My name is Lee Soo Min,

612
00:26:35,330 --> 00:26:36,900
and I'm an actress.

613
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:41,200
I was in that show so long ago
that it's embarrassing to say now.

614
00:26:41,370 --> 00:26:44,440
But yes, I'm the girl from "Boni Hani."

615
00:26:44,800 --> 00:26:47,940
"Boyfriend on Demand" was
a huge hit recently,

616
00:26:48,010 --> 00:26:51,610
so I've been getting recognized
for that instead lately.

617
00:26:51,610 --> 00:26:53,110
It brings a smile to my face.

618
00:26:53,950 --> 00:26:55,280
My nickname, Pitou?

619
00:26:55,350 --> 00:26:57,450
I rescued a cat once,

620
00:26:57,450 --> 00:27:00,550
and I happened to be rewatching
the anime "Hunter X Hunter..."

621
00:27:00,550 --> 00:27:02,020
at the time.

622
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:03,060
In the show,

623
00:27:03,060 --> 00:27:06,530
there's a cat-like character
named Neferpitou.

624
00:27:06,530 --> 00:27:08,090
I considered naming my cat Pitou,

625
00:27:08,090 --> 00:27:09,560
but I went with Bbangyi instead.

626
00:27:09,630 --> 00:27:11,460
Still, the name felt too good to pass up,

627
00:27:11,460 --> 00:27:13,630
so I'm using it as my nickname here.

628
00:27:14,400 --> 00:27:16,670
I've always thought of myself...

629
00:27:16,670 --> 00:27:19,440
as someone
who stands firm in their beliefs.

630
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:21,470
But watching this show made me realize...

631
00:27:21,540 --> 00:27:22,840
that I'm easily swayed.

632
00:27:23,240 --> 00:27:25,480
Every time someone brought up
a different point,

633
00:27:25,580 --> 00:27:28,010
I agreed with them,
and then I turned right around...

634
00:27:28,010 --> 00:27:29,050
and agreed with the next person.

635
00:27:29,050 --> 00:27:31,280
Honestly, I was like that the whole time.

636
00:27:31,420 --> 00:27:34,820
Since I can't claim to be the smartest...

637
00:27:34,820 --> 00:27:37,460
or most knowledgeable person here,

638
00:27:37,620 --> 00:27:39,630
I'll stick to something more realistic.

639
00:27:40,530 --> 00:27:44,160
Something like loyalty.

640
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:47,130
I'm not sure
how much I can share about myself.

641
00:27:47,130 --> 00:27:48,330
Exactly. How much are we allowed to...

642
00:27:48,330 --> 00:27:49,570
Is our age a secret as well?

643
00:27:51,040 --> 00:27:52,170
Then again,

644
00:27:52,170 --> 00:27:55,110
I'm at a point in my life
where I'd rather not reveal my age.

645
00:27:55,980 --> 00:27:57,080
Really?

646
00:27:58,080 --> 00:28:00,880
I mean, I'm happier
to keep the number hidden.

647
00:28:01,310 --> 00:28:03,320
My name is Im Ji Eun,

648
00:28:03,420 --> 00:28:05,420
and I'm a writer...

649
00:28:05,420 --> 00:28:08,760
specializing mostly in essays.

650
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:10,890
People first learned about me...

651
00:28:10,890 --> 00:28:13,260
through my debut essay collection
on feminism.

652
00:28:13,290 --> 00:28:16,430
I guess my perspective
is rooted a lot in classism.

653
00:28:16,560 --> 00:28:19,130
Classism sounds like a heavy word,

654
00:28:19,130 --> 00:28:20,770
and the concept feels kind of abstract.

655
00:28:20,770 --> 00:28:22,400
Back in season one,

656
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:25,100
we were asked
if we'd ever seen a rat in our house.

657
00:28:25,140 --> 00:28:26,410
I actually have.

658
00:28:26,540 --> 00:28:29,910
I feel like even the idea of self-esteem
is rooted in class background.

659
00:28:30,040 --> 00:28:31,880
There are people...

660
00:28:31,880 --> 00:28:34,280
who grew up with a life...

661
00:28:34,280 --> 00:28:36,420
where they could afford to make mistakes.

662
00:28:36,420 --> 00:28:38,690
Meanwhile, there are those
whose single mistake...

663
00:28:38,690 --> 00:28:40,390
would mean serious consequences.

664
00:28:40,950 --> 00:28:43,790
Their attitudes toward life
couldn't be more different,

665
00:28:43,790 --> 00:28:46,630
and even that's tied to class as well.

666
00:28:47,290 --> 00:28:49,830
My nickname is Salt.

667
00:28:50,630 --> 00:28:52,270
I'm all about that frugal life.

668
00:28:52,370 --> 00:28:54,930
I'll show you what kind of survivor I am.

669
00:28:55,330 --> 00:28:57,800
(A consistent and strict idealist)

670
00:28:58,200 --> 00:29:00,210
People from various fields
were featured...

671
00:29:00,210 --> 00:29:01,910
on the first season.

672
00:29:01,910 --> 00:29:03,310
For me, Genie...

673
00:29:04,580 --> 00:29:05,680
I mean,

674
00:29:05,780 --> 00:29:08,410
the Peking duck...

675
00:29:09,550 --> 00:29:13,950
(Genie's speech mission in season 1)

676
00:29:14,590 --> 00:29:17,490
I absolutely love Peking duck,

677
00:29:17,760 --> 00:29:19,090
but I felt so hurt and miserable...

678
00:29:19,090 --> 00:29:21,190
that I bawled,
crying that I wanted Peking duck.

679
00:29:21,690 --> 00:29:26,900
(The Peking duck story that turned heads)

680
00:29:27,500 --> 00:29:29,970
We couldn't be more different,

681
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:31,540
and her story rubbed me the wrong way.

682
00:29:31,600 --> 00:29:34,140
Framing her Peking duck story...

683
00:29:34,140 --> 00:29:36,710
as the most painful moment of her life?

684
00:29:36,840 --> 00:29:38,010
That just made me roll my eyes.

685
00:29:38,140 --> 00:29:40,010
And there was that time...

686
00:29:40,010 --> 00:29:42,320
when an immunity pass
was completely useless.

687
00:29:42,320 --> 00:29:44,350
She dragged it out
until the very last moment...

688
00:29:44,350 --> 00:29:45,950
and had to make a run for it.

689
00:29:46,120 --> 00:29:49,090
(Genie had the power
to save someone from elimination.)

690
00:29:49,490 --> 00:29:52,760
(She dragged it out
until the very last moment.)

691
00:29:52,760 --> 00:29:54,860
(She ended up failing to make the save.)

692
00:29:55,560 --> 00:29:57,860
Who heads out running
with only ten seconds left?

693
00:29:57,860 --> 00:29:59,130
She was just covering her bases.

694
00:29:59,130 --> 00:30:01,630
It was obvious
she wouldn't make it in time.

695
00:30:01,930 --> 00:30:05,440
(A lot of people called Genie out
for her actions.)

696
00:30:06,240 --> 00:30:09,840
In more ways than one,
she got on my nerves.

697
00:30:09,840 --> 00:30:12,040
Frankly, it was hard not to view her...

698
00:30:12,040 --> 00:30:13,710
as selfish.

699
00:30:17,350 --> 00:30:20,720
Hi, I'm back home.

700
00:30:20,750 --> 00:30:21,820
I'm Genie.

701
00:30:22,760 --> 00:30:25,530
I used to have an office job back then,

702
00:30:25,530 --> 00:30:27,890
but now I'm a freelancer
working in broadcasting...

703
00:30:27,890 --> 00:30:29,260
as an actor and entertainer.

704
00:30:29,260 --> 00:30:32,170
Following that show, I was cast...

705
00:30:32,170 --> 00:30:33,530
in two other survival shows,

706
00:30:33,530 --> 00:30:35,970
which got me labeled as someone
obsessed with the genre.

707
00:30:36,040 --> 00:30:38,810
Being able to challenge myself
to do all kinds of things...

708
00:30:38,810 --> 00:30:40,240
is a huge advantage.

709
00:30:40,570 --> 00:30:43,710
I got mocked endlessly
over that Peking duck story.

710
00:30:43,710 --> 00:30:46,010
Still, people around me
started buying me Peking duck,

711
00:30:46,010 --> 00:30:47,180
which I happily devoured.

712
00:30:47,180 --> 00:30:49,250
My speech from that day
keeps showing up...

713
00:30:49,250 --> 00:30:50,950
in short clips online,

714
00:30:50,950 --> 00:30:52,720
and judging by the comments,

715
00:30:52,720 --> 00:30:55,390
people really despised me for it.

716
00:30:55,490 --> 00:30:58,660
People have been targeting me
because of that.

717
00:30:59,130 --> 00:31:00,360
(Practicality 2, Ability 2, Principle 1)

718
00:31:00,360 --> 00:31:01,690
What do they mean?

719
00:31:01,690 --> 00:31:03,860
Practicality, ability, principle?

720
00:31:04,760 --> 00:31:06,530
(Extremely money-driven, standing
as the only Level 2 practical player.)

721
00:31:06,530 --> 00:31:07,700
"Ability."

722
00:31:10,470 --> 00:31:11,570
Can I keep this pen?

723
00:31:14,110 --> 00:31:15,680
No one's answering? I'm taking it then.

724
00:31:16,310 --> 00:31:17,780
(Genie, TV entertainer)

725
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:20,510
I'm going to show them
how having prior experience...

726
00:31:20,510 --> 00:31:22,320
gives you an edge.

727
00:31:22,320 --> 00:31:25,120
I've got to defend the pride
of season one.

728
00:31:25,120 --> 00:31:27,190
"Don't even think you're on my level."

729
00:31:27,190 --> 00:31:29,190
Just let me naturally lead you,

730
00:31:29,190 --> 00:31:30,760
and you follow.

731
00:31:30,760 --> 00:31:32,190
You'll be in good hands.

732
00:31:32,190 --> 00:31:34,160
That's all I want to say.

733
00:31:34,890 --> 00:31:36,130
"Rather not be noticed..."

734
00:31:36,130 --> 00:31:37,300
Wait, is this the place?

735
00:31:39,600 --> 00:31:41,170
- No way.
- Is someone else joining us?

736
00:31:41,530 --> 00:31:42,900
- Well...
- Why don't I get the door?

737
00:31:43,370 --> 00:31:45,440
- Coming.
- No way.

738
00:31:47,570 --> 00:31:48,940
- Hello.
- Hello.

739
00:31:48,940 --> 00:31:50,680
- Hi.
- Hello.

740
00:31:50,680 --> 00:31:52,040
Hello.

741
00:31:52,280 --> 00:31:53,950
- Wait, aren't you...
- Hello.

742
00:31:54,250 --> 00:31:55,410
- Right?
- That's right.

743
00:31:55,410 --> 00:31:56,950
(They immediately recognize Genie.)

744
00:31:56,950 --> 00:31:58,920
Yes, it's me. You probably watched...

745
00:31:58,920 --> 00:32:02,090
- season one before coming here.
- Right.

746
00:32:02,660 --> 00:32:04,160
- I mean...
- I wonder why the three of us...

747
00:32:04,520 --> 00:32:05,660
are a team.

748
00:32:05,660 --> 00:32:07,230
This place is so pretty.

749
00:32:07,260 --> 00:32:09,930
We took the time to briefly...

750
00:32:10,030 --> 00:32:11,630
introduce ourselves,

751
00:32:11,630 --> 00:32:12,770
but you don't really need to.

752
00:32:12,770 --> 00:32:13,900
I know, she's...

753
00:32:14,230 --> 00:32:16,340
Her entrance is
all the introduction she needs.

754
00:32:17,040 --> 00:32:19,170
How can you do this to me?

755
00:32:19,670 --> 00:32:21,370
I didn't hold back at all...

756
00:32:21,410 --> 00:32:23,380
about how much I hated...

757
00:32:23,380 --> 00:32:24,540
(Salt, Author)

758
00:32:24,540 --> 00:32:25,850
Genie.

759
00:32:25,910 --> 00:32:28,410
How can you do something so evil...

760
00:32:28,410 --> 00:32:32,080
as putting me in the same room as her?

761
00:32:32,250 --> 00:32:34,390
When she walked through the door...

762
00:32:34,520 --> 00:32:38,320
I took vegetable melatonin yesterday,

763
00:32:38,320 --> 00:32:41,930
which can cause nightmares
as a side effect.

764
00:32:41,960 --> 00:32:43,260
Could what I'm experiencing...

765
00:32:43,560 --> 00:32:45,630
be a nightmare caused by it?

766
00:32:45,800 --> 00:32:47,970
What was she doing here?

767
00:32:48,500 --> 00:32:50,570
She was actually here, and both of them...

768
00:32:50,570 --> 00:32:52,170
were wearing tweed jackets...

769
00:32:52,170 --> 00:32:55,040
as if they were mocking me.

770
00:32:55,070 --> 00:32:57,340
Talk about death by fashion.

771
00:32:57,540 --> 00:32:58,910
I mean,

772
00:32:58,910 --> 00:33:00,180
my nickname is Salt,

773
00:33:00,180 --> 00:33:02,880
but I didn't want to come across as cheap.

774
00:33:03,320 --> 00:33:04,880
I was completely thrown off.

775
00:33:06,550 --> 00:33:08,520
I stayed up late...

776
00:33:08,520 --> 00:33:10,520
watching season one
of The Community last night.

777
00:33:10,520 --> 00:33:11,620
What?

778
00:33:11,660 --> 00:33:14,730
It didn't seem real.
I felt like I was dreaming.

779
00:33:15,060 --> 00:33:16,560
Judging by what I saw on season one,

780
00:33:16,600 --> 00:33:17,800
I didn't think we'd get along.

781
00:33:18,160 --> 00:33:20,930
She always seemed to choose
the opposite of what I chose,

782
00:33:20,930 --> 00:33:24,270
so I worried about how we'd get along.

783
00:33:24,640 --> 00:33:26,170
I wonder what this is.

784
00:33:27,470 --> 00:33:28,570
A stamp?

785
00:33:30,310 --> 00:33:31,940
Is this for when we vote?

786
00:33:34,680 --> 00:33:37,150
- Experience working at its best.
- Experience working at its best.

787
00:33:37,350 --> 00:33:40,520
This is exactly why
having experience is valued.

788
00:33:40,520 --> 00:33:42,360
- Tell me about it.
- No way.

789
00:33:42,360 --> 00:33:45,660
It feels embarrassing to be praised
for something like this.

790
00:33:46,190 --> 00:33:47,990
I didn't know the two.

791
00:33:48,090 --> 00:33:51,830
But they seemed to be nice people
from the start.

792
00:33:51,900 --> 00:33:53,870
They're good to be with as they're nice,

793
00:33:53,900 --> 00:33:56,170
but I was quite worried about...

794
00:33:56,170 --> 00:33:58,440
whether these herbivores would be okay.

795
00:33:58,970 --> 00:34:01,170
You must be quiet in general, Paul.

796
00:34:01,170 --> 00:34:02,310
Sorry? No.

797
00:34:02,310 --> 00:34:03,380
- No?
- No.

798
00:34:03,380 --> 00:34:05,440
Paul doesn't seem to be one of us.

799
00:34:05,440 --> 00:34:06,450
- That could be the case.
- No.

800
00:34:06,450 --> 00:34:08,210
- In my opinion,
- He's being too strategic.

801
00:34:08,210 --> 00:34:10,650
he's like that on the outside,
but he's full of air inside.

802
00:34:10,650 --> 00:34:11,750
Right.

803
00:34:11,850 --> 00:34:14,020
- I'm an airhead.
- He's full of air.

804
00:34:15,320 --> 00:34:19,560
I strongly thought that these people
hadn't been on a survival show before.

805
00:34:19,560 --> 00:34:21,560
"This is the calm before the storm."

806
00:34:21,560 --> 00:34:25,460
It felt like they were trying to
make me the villain.

807
00:34:25,460 --> 00:34:27,330
They were all bubbly,

808
00:34:27,330 --> 00:34:29,870
and it was hard to bear that.

809
00:34:29,870 --> 00:34:33,010
"Will I be able to compete
in a team game with these people?"

810
00:34:33,010 --> 00:34:35,140
It was my first time feeling
so drained in a survival show.

811
00:34:35,140 --> 00:34:36,540
It was so tough.

812
00:34:37,210 --> 00:34:38,310
I'm...

813
00:34:39,480 --> 00:34:40,580
- Gosh!
- Gosh!

814
00:34:40,580 --> 00:34:41,710
Something's playing.

815
00:34:42,780 --> 00:34:45,520
All residents have moved in.

816
00:34:46,020 --> 00:34:49,020
In the resource storage
within the dormitory you have moved into,

817
00:34:49,490 --> 00:34:53,730
there are three types of resources
that will be used here.

818
00:34:54,460 --> 00:34:55,900
From now on,

819
00:34:56,060 --> 00:34:58,530
I will explain the three types
of resources in Community Town.

820
00:34:58,660 --> 00:35:02,840
They are diamonds, lives, and credits.

821
00:35:03,770 --> 00:35:05,500
I'll start with the diamonds.

822
00:35:05,770 --> 00:35:08,340
Diamonds are equivalent
to the prize money of the residents.

823
00:35:08,570 --> 00:35:09,710
- Diamonds.
- Right.

824
00:35:09,710 --> 00:35:11,610
The total prize money
of 200,000 dollars...

825
00:35:11,610 --> 00:35:13,710
will be based on...

826
00:35:13,710 --> 00:35:16,220
the number of diamonds acquired
at the end of the residency.

827
00:35:16,320 --> 00:35:21,550
Each resident will earn the prize money
based on the number of diamonds acquired.

828
00:35:21,690 --> 00:35:24,720
- They're like chips.
- There are currently 200 diamonds.

829
00:35:24,920 --> 00:35:28,560
- Thus, 1 diamond is worth 1,000 dollars.
- It's 1,000 dollars.

830
00:35:29,100 --> 00:35:30,900
Next are the lives.

831
00:35:31,160 --> 00:35:33,930
Life represents the lives
of the residents.

832
00:35:34,770 --> 00:35:38,140
At the time when all official activities
come to an end,

833
00:35:38,440 --> 00:35:40,740
each of you must submit one life.

834
00:35:41,710 --> 00:35:45,240
When a resident fails to submit
a life at that moment,

835
00:35:45,580 --> 00:35:47,710
that resident will be removed immediately.

836
00:35:48,450 --> 00:35:49,980
When removed,

837
00:35:49,980 --> 00:35:53,350
the acquired diamonds may not be
earned as prize money.

838
00:35:53,890 --> 00:35:57,690
However, when there is one resident left
in a group,

839
00:35:57,690 --> 00:36:00,960
that resident will not be removed
even with zero lives.

840
00:36:00,960 --> 00:36:02,560
Oh, I see!

841
00:36:02,800 --> 00:36:04,730
Next are the credits.

842
00:36:04,730 --> 00:36:07,730
Credits represent the opportunities
of the residents.

843
00:36:07,870 --> 00:36:10,870
All activities in Community Town...

844
00:36:10,970 --> 00:36:12,510
require credits.

845
00:36:12,870 --> 00:36:15,440
More credits will provide...

846
00:36:15,440 --> 00:36:18,140
more choices for the residents.

847
00:36:18,780 --> 00:36:24,150
For the residents to collect the diamonds
as prize money,

848
00:36:24,280 --> 00:36:28,320
lives must be collected to be able
to survive until the end.

849
00:36:28,390 --> 00:36:29,990
In this whole process,

850
00:36:30,120 --> 00:36:32,220
credits will be needed.

851
00:36:32,860 --> 00:36:36,630
I wish all of you the best of luck.

852
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:39,900
For more details,

853
00:36:39,900 --> 00:36:43,440
open the rule card envelope
placed on the table.

854
00:36:44,040 --> 00:36:45,640
- Can we open this now?
- Yes.

855
00:36:45,910 --> 00:36:47,770
We can open the envelope, right?

856
00:36:47,770 --> 00:36:49,380
- I'll open it.
- Right.

857
00:36:50,910 --> 00:36:53,050
We have 200 diamonds,

858
00:36:53,050 --> 00:36:54,810
- eight lives,
- Eight lives,

859
00:36:54,980 --> 00:36:56,480
- and 3 credits.
- and 3 credits.

860
00:36:57,720 --> 00:36:59,720
If there are 8 lives
when there are 6 of us,

861
00:36:59,720 --> 00:37:01,320
we'll be left with 2 if we use 1 each.

862
00:37:01,320 --> 00:37:02,790
Right. That's how it will be.

863
00:37:02,820 --> 00:37:05,460
I'm sure we'll have to use our diamonds
to buy lives in the future.

864
00:37:05,460 --> 00:37:06,660
I believe so.

865
00:37:06,660 --> 00:37:08,060
That will bring down the prize money.

866
00:37:08,460 --> 00:37:09,660
There are eight lives?

867
00:37:09,660 --> 00:37:10,860
But there are six of us.

868
00:37:10,860 --> 00:37:13,900
Does that mean we're safe
only for a single day?

869
00:37:14,030 --> 00:37:15,400
One must...

870
00:37:15,400 --> 00:37:17,040
be given a couple of days to live...

871
00:37:17,040 --> 00:37:18,300
to be able to think about something else.

872
00:37:18,300 --> 00:37:20,570
We might end up dying the next day.

873
00:37:20,670 --> 00:37:23,210
I thought that I had to start moving
right away...

874
00:37:23,210 --> 00:37:26,310
to get more lives.

875
00:37:26,310 --> 00:37:28,950
Life stood out to me the most.

876
00:37:28,980 --> 00:37:30,320
For us to survive another day,

877
00:37:30,320 --> 00:37:32,950
we had to secure four more.

878
00:37:33,150 --> 00:37:36,860
I thought about how we could
secure more lives.

879
00:37:37,320 --> 00:37:40,190
- All of our numbers are here.
- Right.

880
00:37:40,690 --> 00:37:42,860
- Lives are the most important.
- Right.

881
00:37:42,860 --> 00:37:44,230
- We can live for a day.
- Right.

882
00:37:44,360 --> 00:37:46,100
- One day.
- Two must be removed the next day.

883
00:37:46,100 --> 00:37:48,300
We have to earn two more today...

884
00:37:48,470 --> 00:37:50,300
- for all of us to survive the next day.
- Or...

885
00:37:50,300 --> 00:37:51,770
What if there are 2 lives left
when there are 3 of us?

886
00:37:51,770 --> 00:37:53,810
- Right.
- Who must leave?

887
00:37:54,040 --> 00:37:55,940
Must the three of us fight each other?

888
00:37:55,940 --> 00:37:57,310
- That's how it's structured.
- Right.

889
00:37:57,340 --> 00:37:58,680
- It's because...
- Right.

890
00:37:58,810 --> 00:38:01,810
we need 3 lives each day
for the 3 of us to survive.

891
00:38:02,010 --> 00:38:03,550
If we are to survive as a team,

892
00:38:03,550 --> 00:38:06,990
we are to know that
it's going to be tough.

893
00:38:06,990 --> 00:38:09,190
We must know what we can do
with the cards.

894
00:38:11,760 --> 00:38:14,160
- Thirty?
- Thirty lives right now?

895
00:38:14,430 --> 00:38:16,430
We have zero diamonds.

896
00:38:16,430 --> 00:38:18,030
Should we open it?

897
00:38:18,060 --> 00:38:19,370
Was that the end of the explanation?

898
00:38:19,370 --> 00:38:21,430
(They will have 6 lives left
after surviving until the end.)

899
00:38:22,070 --> 00:38:23,500
The 200,000 dollars...

900
00:38:23,500 --> 00:38:25,170
That was exactly what was said.

901
00:38:25,170 --> 00:38:28,170
More credits will provide
more choices for the residents.

902
00:38:28,210 --> 00:38:29,240
- That's it.
- Okay.

903
00:38:29,510 --> 00:38:30,540
It's a summary...

904
00:38:30,540 --> 00:38:32,180
- what was said.
- It's just like the announcement.

905
00:38:32,180 --> 00:38:33,880
The voting paper is here.

906
00:38:33,880 --> 00:38:35,480
- Voting paper?
- It's just for us.

907
00:38:35,480 --> 00:38:36,650
Will we pick a leader?

908
00:38:36,650 --> 00:38:37,950
- It's voting paper.
- Right.

909
00:38:37,950 --> 00:38:39,490
We must submit it.

910
00:38:40,290 --> 00:38:41,820
- Earlier,
- Right.

911
00:38:41,920 --> 00:38:43,120
we were told that the last one left...

912
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:45,020
will not get removed
even with no lives left.

913
00:38:45,020 --> 00:38:47,230
Must the three of us
compete against each other?

914
00:38:48,490 --> 00:38:49,500
What?

915
00:38:49,530 --> 00:38:50,560
It says...

916
00:38:50,560 --> 00:38:53,570
that's only for that last one left.

917
00:38:53,570 --> 00:38:55,570
If one of us survives...

918
00:39:00,340 --> 00:39:02,940
The leader will be chosen now.

919
00:39:02,980 --> 00:39:05,040
- You will be given 30 minutes...
- Leader?

920
00:39:05,180 --> 00:39:07,150
to discuss freely...

921
00:39:07,210 --> 00:39:08,580
and choose a leader.

922
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:09,880
- Within our team?
- Within our team.

923
00:39:09,880 --> 00:39:11,520
Once a resident becomes a leader,

924
00:39:11,520 --> 00:39:13,050
the leader will continue
to be the leader...

925
00:39:13,050 --> 00:39:14,450
unless a revote is decided.

926
00:39:14,790 --> 00:39:16,490
The revote for a leader...

927
00:39:16,490 --> 00:39:18,890
can be called out every morning.

928
00:39:19,690 --> 00:39:20,930
Every morning?

929
00:39:21,490 --> 00:39:23,860
- There are just three of us.
- We must choose a leader.

930
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:25,030
- Right.
- Right.

931
00:39:25,560 --> 00:39:26,870
We have 30 minutes left.

932
00:39:26,900 --> 00:39:29,440
Is there anyone who wishes
to be the leader?

933
00:39:29,570 --> 00:39:30,770
Anyone who wishes to be the leader?

934
00:39:30,770 --> 00:39:33,510
Well, I tend to be the leader often.

935
00:39:34,340 --> 00:39:35,570
I want to become the leader as well.

936
00:39:35,570 --> 00:39:36,640
- Great.
- Really?

937
00:39:37,380 --> 00:39:38,380
Great.

938
00:39:38,380 --> 00:39:39,680
What about you, Boss?

939
00:39:39,680 --> 00:39:41,410
I don't have a strong desire
to become one,

940
00:39:41,510 --> 00:39:43,120
- but I will accept that role.
- You will accept that role?

941
00:39:43,480 --> 00:39:45,080
- I thought she really wanted the role.
- Me?

942
00:39:45,080 --> 00:39:46,120
- Because I'm Boss?
- Right.

943
00:39:46,120 --> 00:39:47,720
- Because she's Boss?
- Her name says she's a leader?

944
00:39:47,720 --> 00:39:49,820
- Right.
- Because of the name "Boss?"

945
00:39:49,960 --> 00:39:53,530
- We must have a specific goal set...
- Right.

946
00:39:53,530 --> 00:39:55,190
for us to decide.

947
00:39:55,190 --> 00:39:56,800
Simply put,

948
00:39:56,800 --> 00:39:59,430
it could be, "Let's make a lot of money."

949
00:39:59,430 --> 00:40:01,270
Or it could be,
"Let's collect a lot of lives..."

950
00:40:01,270 --> 00:40:03,900
"and stay alive for a long time."

951
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,240
Personally, I think lives...

952
00:40:06,240 --> 00:40:07,510
should be the first priority.

953
00:40:08,310 --> 00:40:09,440
The second priority should be credits,

954
00:40:09,440 --> 00:40:10,810
and the third priority
should be prize money.

955
00:40:10,840 --> 00:40:11,980
It's the same for me.

956
00:40:12,180 --> 00:40:13,750
Right. I was going to say that.

957
00:40:14,010 --> 00:40:17,550
We can decide if we want to
go for more prize money...

958
00:40:17,550 --> 00:40:18,950
even if it requires sacrificing our lives.

959
00:40:19,050 --> 00:40:21,590
This is what I mean by saying that.

960
00:40:21,590 --> 00:40:25,120
I obviously want everyone to survive,

961
00:40:25,120 --> 00:40:26,430
but to me,

962
00:40:26,430 --> 00:40:28,690
my life is more precious
than the prize money.

963
00:40:28,690 --> 00:40:30,030
- Your life?
- Right.

964
00:40:30,030 --> 00:40:31,330
- I want to survive.
- I see.

965
00:40:31,330 --> 00:40:32,900
When we decide on a leader,

966
00:40:32,900 --> 00:40:35,930
I want the leader to protect my life
more than my money.

967
00:40:35,930 --> 00:40:36,970
Right.

968
00:40:36,970 --> 00:40:39,100
A leader must be...

969
00:40:39,200 --> 00:40:40,570
quite ambitious...

970
00:40:40,740 --> 00:40:42,670
to be able to keep us alive.

971
00:40:42,670 --> 00:40:44,940
- Right.
- I wish it to be someone...

972
00:40:44,940 --> 00:40:47,380
who is talented in that area.

973
00:40:47,910 --> 00:40:49,850
Instead of me being the leader,

974
00:40:50,150 --> 00:40:52,050
I think Luna, who is more calculative...

975
00:40:52,050 --> 00:40:53,520
- "Calculative?"
- Oh, no.

976
00:40:53,520 --> 00:40:54,590
I'm sorry.

977
00:40:54,920 --> 00:40:56,960
- I meant you were...
- When did I form that image?

978
00:40:56,960 --> 00:41:00,460
I'm not someone
who is competition-oriented.

979
00:41:00,530 --> 00:41:03,800
That was why I recommended Luna
to become our leader.

980
00:41:04,430 --> 00:41:05,560
Thank you.

981
00:41:05,860 --> 00:41:07,270
If I am to recommend myself,

982
00:41:07,830 --> 00:41:09,570
I have a lot of experience...

983
00:41:09,570 --> 00:41:11,300
with being a leader.

984
00:41:11,370 --> 00:41:13,270
I tend to volunteer to be the leader...

985
00:41:13,270 --> 00:41:14,940
in gatherings.

986
00:41:15,210 --> 00:41:16,840
I'm quite competitive as well.

987
00:41:17,080 --> 00:41:18,140
Right.

988
00:41:18,140 --> 00:41:21,010
When there are other teams,

989
00:41:21,010 --> 00:41:23,920
I tend to try my best...

990
00:41:23,920 --> 00:41:25,980
- to protect my team.
- Great.

991
00:41:27,090 --> 00:41:29,660
We chose Luna.

992
00:41:29,720 --> 00:41:31,290
It was by unanimous consent.

993
00:41:31,390 --> 00:41:33,130
In a community,

994
00:41:33,130 --> 00:41:35,030
there are leaders and followers.

995
00:41:35,060 --> 00:41:39,230
I believe the followers
must fully respect the leader's opinion.

996
00:41:39,230 --> 00:41:42,570
Luna expressed herself well,
so I believed she deserved that role.

997
00:41:43,240 --> 00:41:45,270
I want to become the leader, though.

998
00:41:45,270 --> 00:41:47,710
I will take that role if I get the chance.

999
00:41:47,810 --> 00:41:50,810
At a certain moment,
I will ask for a revote.

1000
00:41:50,810 --> 00:41:52,880
"Others must also be given a chance."

1001
00:41:52,880 --> 00:41:56,820
"I want to be given that chance."
I'm ready to make that claim.

1002
00:41:56,820 --> 00:42:00,450
I thought about wanting to become
the leader even when I was at home.

1003
00:42:01,290 --> 00:42:04,560
Once I become the leader,
I will be given more information.

1004
00:42:04,560 --> 00:42:06,890
I did volunteer to become the leader,

1005
00:42:06,890 --> 00:42:08,730
thinking that I might be given
at least one more life.

1006
00:42:08,790 --> 00:42:10,430
I thought it was a role
that I had to take.

1007
00:42:10,430 --> 00:42:12,230
(Luna, Team White leader)

1008
00:42:12,500 --> 00:42:13,830
Seeing you, Luna,

1009
00:42:13,830 --> 00:42:16,400
I was reminded of Genie
out of the Season One characters.

1010
00:42:16,400 --> 00:42:17,440
- Right.
- Really?

1011
00:42:17,440 --> 00:42:19,370
- Yes.
- I'm not that type.

1012
00:42:19,370 --> 00:42:22,040
Genie went over the line.

1013
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:23,810
- She went over the line?
- Right.

1014
00:42:29,280 --> 00:42:30,650
If the leader...

1015
00:42:31,650 --> 00:42:33,990
doesn't have to be the leader...

1016
00:42:33,990 --> 00:42:35,450
until the very end,

1017
00:42:35,450 --> 00:42:36,720
how about we each take turns?

1018
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:38,560
We have a lot of time anyway.

1019
00:42:38,560 --> 00:42:39,760
- Sounds good.
- Right.

1020
00:42:39,760 --> 00:42:41,830
Do any of you want to go first?

1021
00:42:41,830 --> 00:42:43,360
No.
The one with experience should go first.

1022
00:42:43,360 --> 00:42:44,400
The one with experience.

1023
00:42:44,400 --> 00:42:46,370
- That will make me feel comfortable.
- If you're okay with that.

1024
00:42:46,470 --> 00:42:48,530
This is a completely new game,

1025
00:42:48,530 --> 00:42:49,900
- so it won't mean much.
- Right.

1026
00:42:49,900 --> 00:42:52,200
If you feel comfortable with that,
I'll give it a try.

1027
00:42:52,200 --> 00:42:54,170
- You two can each take turns.
- Right.

1028
00:42:54,170 --> 00:42:55,840
- Right. Sounds good.
- Okay.

1029
00:42:56,140 --> 00:42:58,140
Being the leader...

1030
00:42:58,680 --> 00:43:00,680
out of three kids?

1031
00:43:00,750 --> 00:43:02,450
It didn't feel that significant.

1032
00:43:02,510 --> 00:43:06,080
Being the leader of three
didn't seem like a big advantage.

1033
00:43:06,080 --> 00:43:08,320
But they wanted me
to be the leader for the first day.

1034
00:43:08,320 --> 00:43:11,160
I would also ask the one
from the previous season to go.

1035
00:43:11,160 --> 00:43:13,360
I just went, "All right, then."

1036
00:43:13,730 --> 00:43:15,530
The number three,

1037
00:43:15,660 --> 00:43:20,430
based on my girls' middle school
and high school days,

1038
00:43:20,430 --> 00:43:23,140
was a number that always caused disorder.

1039
00:43:23,170 --> 00:43:25,870
If the other two team up
and vote against me,

1040
00:43:25,870 --> 00:43:27,340
I'll get kicked out right away.

1041
00:43:27,340 --> 00:43:29,270
There seems to be a system of...

1042
00:43:29,270 --> 00:43:31,540
"It's every man for himself."

1043
00:43:31,540 --> 00:43:33,580
When there is one survivor in the team,

1044
00:43:33,680 --> 00:43:35,480
lives become useless.

1045
00:43:36,180 --> 00:43:38,320
I get those thoughts...

1046
00:43:38,320 --> 00:43:39,590
from the number three.

1047
00:43:39,950 --> 00:43:42,490
I think I'm in the middle.

1048
00:43:42,520 --> 00:43:44,320
The two seem to be...

1049
00:43:44,490 --> 00:43:46,760
quite distant from each other.

1050
00:43:46,760 --> 00:43:49,560
Ultimately, it'll be easier
for the only one left in the group,

1051
00:43:49,560 --> 00:43:52,030
as lives won't be needed at all.

1052
00:43:52,030 --> 00:43:54,400
If this wasn't a team game...

1053
00:43:54,400 --> 00:43:57,370
and it was a battle between
the three of us,

1054
00:43:57,940 --> 00:44:01,710
I would feel really scared.

1055
00:44:01,710 --> 00:44:06,850
I had to see what rights
and privileges the leader was given.

1056
00:44:06,850 --> 00:44:09,780
That was why I asked us
to each take turns.

1057
00:44:11,250 --> 00:44:12,550
Who will become the leader?

1058
00:44:12,850 --> 00:44:14,590
What rights will the leader be given?

1059
00:44:14,590 --> 00:44:17,890
How will those resources be distributed?

1060
00:44:17,890 --> 00:44:21,160
I'm fine with letting someone else
become the leader,

1061
00:44:21,290 --> 00:44:22,860
but whoever the leader is,

1062
00:44:22,860 --> 00:44:25,530
I wish for our team to focus
on collecting more lives.

1063
00:44:25,530 --> 00:44:27,430
Right. We must focus on that.

1064
00:44:28,070 --> 00:44:29,600
- I just wish the leader won't want to...
- Yes.

1065
00:44:30,040 --> 00:44:31,900
survive on his own and let the others die.

1066
00:44:31,900 --> 00:44:35,840
- Becoming the leader of us...
- Right.

1067
00:44:36,140 --> 00:44:38,240
- should mean we're all on the same side.
- Right.

1068
00:44:38,340 --> 00:44:40,610
I believe our team is quite competitive
when it comes to brain power.

1069
00:44:41,080 --> 00:44:43,780
- I don't see ourselves leaving early.
- Right.

1070
00:44:43,780 --> 00:44:45,220
- We have a good chance.
- Right.

1071
00:44:45,220 --> 00:44:46,420
Until the end,

1072
00:44:46,420 --> 00:44:48,320
- we should try to survive...
- Right.

1073
00:44:48,550 --> 00:44:50,490
- to the best of our abilities.
- Right.

1074
00:44:51,090 --> 00:44:52,620
- I agree.
- Right.

1075
00:44:52,790 --> 00:44:55,290
So our leader is Maillard?

1076
00:44:55,790 --> 00:44:57,130
It should be Maillard.

1077
00:44:57,130 --> 00:44:59,160
He's someone
who has a bit of fighting spirit.

1078
00:44:59,160 --> 00:45:00,500
I like that beard.

1079
00:45:00,500 --> 00:45:01,970
The beard? It's the beard of a leader.

1080
00:45:01,970 --> 00:45:03,070
- Right.
- He has the face of a leader.

1081
00:45:03,070 --> 00:45:04,070
Wherever he goes,

1082
00:45:04,070 --> 00:45:06,070
- he won't give in.
- He won't give in.

1083
00:45:06,070 --> 00:45:08,070
- So Maillard will be our leader.
- I agree.

1084
00:45:08,070 --> 00:45:09,640
- I'll try being a leader for a day.
- Right.

1085
00:45:09,640 --> 00:45:11,310
- Right.
- We'll see how it goes.

1086
00:45:11,310 --> 00:45:12,680
- So that's decided.
- Right.

1087
00:45:12,910 --> 00:45:14,380
It got decided all of a sudden.

1088
00:45:14,380 --> 00:45:16,280
My teammates said it was
because of my beard.

1089
00:45:16,820 --> 00:45:19,150
My teammates all pretend to be humble,

1090
00:45:19,150 --> 00:45:20,950
but they are people
who can take this role.

1091
00:45:21,150 --> 00:45:22,220
We're all the same.

1092
00:45:22,220 --> 00:45:24,260
It seems like we're all
trying to read the room.

1093
00:45:24,820 --> 00:45:26,930
The leader selection is over.

1094
00:45:27,360 --> 00:45:30,000
Please head to the Community Center...

1095
00:45:30,330 --> 00:45:32,230
to register the leader of your group.

1096
00:45:34,670 --> 00:45:35,730
It's Genie.

1097
00:45:36,540 --> 00:45:39,370
Genie, congratulations
on becoming the leader.

1098
00:45:39,810 --> 00:45:42,640
Pick up the resource storage key...

1099
00:45:42,940 --> 00:45:45,480
and put on the leader badge.

1100
00:45:46,310 --> 00:45:48,710
This must be used
for the thing in our room.

1101
00:45:48,710 --> 00:45:50,680
- The resource storage.
- Right.

1102
00:45:50,950 --> 00:45:54,390
I understand why people feel proud
of becoming a leader of any minor group.

1103
00:45:54,390 --> 00:45:57,190
Putting on a badge...

1104
00:45:57,190 --> 00:46:00,230
made me feel somewhat proud.

1105
00:46:00,630 --> 00:46:01,990
This is what it feels like right now.

1106
00:46:01,990 --> 00:46:03,660
When I was in elementary school,

1107
00:46:04,030 --> 00:46:07,370
I dreamt of these moments
while taking a nap after piano class.

1108
00:46:07,630 --> 00:46:09,470
- What kind of dream is that?
- Why is it so specific?

1109
00:46:09,630 --> 00:46:10,640
- What?
- What?

1110
00:46:10,640 --> 00:46:11,800
What was that?

1111
00:46:11,800 --> 00:46:13,970
You have N in your MBTI, don't you?

1112
00:46:13,970 --> 00:46:14,970
Yes.

1113
00:46:15,240 --> 00:46:16,480
A total intuitive.

1114
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:17,510
- A total intuitive.
- Right.

1115
00:46:17,510 --> 00:46:19,340
This must be it. It looks pretty.

1116
00:46:19,340 --> 00:46:21,110
We must be the first group here.

1117
00:46:21,110 --> 00:46:22,110
Really?

1118
00:46:23,950 --> 00:46:26,750
(Town Hall)

1119
00:46:26,750 --> 00:46:29,990
- Gosh, the menu brings back memories.
- Gosh!

1120
00:46:31,290 --> 00:46:32,420
"Supermarket Rules."

1121
00:46:32,420 --> 00:46:35,560
"Products must be purchased with
the number of diamonds Black asks for."

1122
00:46:35,560 --> 00:46:37,100
- Black?
- Who is Black?

1123
00:46:37,100 --> 00:46:38,800
- Is someone here?
- I guess so.

1124
00:46:39,400 --> 00:46:40,870
Gosh, they look good.

1125
00:46:41,430 --> 00:46:42,430
I'm hungry.

1126
00:46:42,670 --> 00:46:45,040
- Should we sit?
- They should be coming.

1127
00:46:45,040 --> 00:46:47,110
Let's take the best seats.

1128
00:46:47,570 --> 00:46:49,270
This place looks really nice.

1129
00:46:49,940 --> 00:46:52,180
I wonder why we were chosen to be
Team Blue.

1130
00:46:52,480 --> 00:46:55,180
I don't think we were put together
based on our teamwork.

1131
00:46:56,880 --> 00:46:59,450
I thought you were an intuitive.

1132
00:46:59,450 --> 00:47:00,690
Just now...

1133
00:47:00,690 --> 00:47:02,650
- That hurt me a bit.
- Just now...

1134
00:47:02,650 --> 00:47:04,420
- I didn't mean it like that.
- What you just said sounded like,

1135
00:47:04,420 --> 00:47:05,860
"I don't think I'll get along with you."

1136
00:47:05,860 --> 00:47:07,160
- Gosh.
- No.

1137
00:47:07,830 --> 00:47:10,260
- "It's every man for himself."
- After having a conversation,

1138
00:47:10,260 --> 00:47:11,700
we all felt different.

1139
00:47:12,460 --> 00:47:14,070
You felt that right away?

1140
00:47:14,330 --> 00:47:15,670
- There are people inside.
- Right.

1141
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:19,000
Hey!

1142
00:47:20,770 --> 00:47:22,840
- Hello.
- Hello.

1143
00:47:22,840 --> 00:47:25,040
- Hello.
- Hello.

1144
00:47:25,040 --> 00:47:26,980
- Shall we sit first?
- Right.

1145
00:47:26,980 --> 00:47:28,250
- Take a seat.
- Thank you.

1146
00:47:28,610 --> 00:47:29,780
We are Team Red.

1147
00:47:29,780 --> 00:47:31,950
- Right.
- We are Team Blue.

1148
00:47:31,950 --> 00:47:33,390
You're the leader.

1149
00:47:33,690 --> 00:47:36,560
- Could we see your nicknames?
- Right.

1150
00:47:36,560 --> 00:47:39,620
- I'm Salt.
- Salt.

1151
00:47:39,890 --> 00:47:41,790
Three men came in.

1152
00:47:42,090 --> 00:47:44,260
The one in front had a beard!

1153
00:47:44,630 --> 00:47:47,670
I felt afraid. I felt overwhelmed.

1154
00:47:48,330 --> 00:47:50,140
They looked like mathematicians.

1155
00:47:50,140 --> 00:47:52,770
The three of us looked like kids,

1156
00:47:52,770 --> 00:47:55,910
and they felt like game freaks.

1157
00:47:55,910 --> 00:47:59,640
If there's a deathmatch between our teams,
we are going to get destroyed.

1158
00:47:59,640 --> 00:48:01,480
That was the first thought I got.

1159
00:48:02,310 --> 00:48:04,980
Team Red was in my prediction.

1160
00:48:04,980 --> 00:48:06,520
I kept seeing...

1161
00:48:06,820 --> 00:48:10,120
a mix of white, blue, and red inside.

1162
00:48:10,120 --> 00:48:11,820
I expected to be 3 teams of 3.

1163
00:48:11,820 --> 00:48:14,790
That was my prediction,
and a group of three came in.

1164
00:48:14,790 --> 00:48:18,200
I was sitting there thinking,
"As expected."

1165
00:48:18,200 --> 00:48:20,430
But Black came in on his own
out of nowhere.

1166
00:48:21,130 --> 00:48:23,130
"I didn't expect this at all."

1167
00:48:23,700 --> 00:48:25,600
- Hello.
- Hello.

1168
00:48:25,600 --> 00:48:27,040
- Hello.
- Hello.

1169
00:48:27,040 --> 00:48:28,440
- Black?
- He's Black.

1170
00:48:28,440 --> 00:48:29,810
He's Black?

1171
00:48:29,810 --> 00:48:31,110
- The owner of the supermarket?
- The owner of the supermarket.

1172
00:48:31,110 --> 00:48:33,250
Right. I'm the merchant.

1173
00:48:33,250 --> 00:48:34,780
- I see!
- I see!

1174
00:48:35,280 --> 00:48:36,280
- Hello.
- Gosh!

1175
00:48:36,680 --> 00:48:39,720
Hello. I'm a businessman
and TV personality.

1176
00:48:39,720 --> 00:48:41,250
My name is Jung Jae Ho.

1177
00:48:41,350 --> 00:48:43,490
My nickname is Vector.

1178
00:48:43,490 --> 00:48:46,190
Vector means speed.

1179
00:48:46,190 --> 00:48:49,830
As there's a certain direction,
there's a vector value.

1180
00:48:49,930 --> 00:48:53,300
To represent me as someone
charging endlessly toward a goal,

1181
00:48:53,300 --> 00:48:57,000
I decided to go with the name "Vector."

1182
00:48:57,470 --> 00:48:59,670
Your color is black.

1183
00:48:59,670 --> 00:49:03,440
As black is a group of one,
you naturally become the leader.

1184
00:49:03,440 --> 00:49:07,280
Team Black will become the merchant
of Community Town.

1185
00:49:07,480 --> 00:49:08,480
Merchant?

1186
00:49:08,480 --> 00:49:12,180
The merchant decides the prices
of the items in the supermarket.

1187
00:49:12,620 --> 00:49:15,320
The prize money is earned
by selling those items.

1188
00:49:17,890 --> 00:49:19,560
The first time I was on TV...

1189
00:49:19,560 --> 00:49:21,430
was through a show called "Heart Signal."

1190
00:49:21,430 --> 00:49:23,730
I was also in a show called
"The Black Sheep Game."

1191
00:49:23,730 --> 00:49:25,000
Recently, I was in "Synchro Game."

1192
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:26,730
I became the winner
after winning all four rounds.

1193
00:49:26,730 --> 00:49:29,030
I believe my decision-making
is faster than others.

1194
00:49:29,030 --> 00:49:31,170
I believe I can move quickly...

1195
00:49:31,170 --> 00:49:33,770
after making wise decisions.

1196
00:49:33,770 --> 00:49:36,070
Are you alone in your team?

1197
00:49:36,070 --> 00:49:37,640
So you're the leader.

1198
00:49:37,640 --> 00:49:39,510
- Right. I have the leader badge.
- He's alone.

1199
00:49:40,110 --> 00:49:41,180
You're alone?

1200
00:49:41,480 --> 00:49:42,480
I'm alone.

1201
00:49:43,520 --> 00:49:44,850
Do you use a room alone?

1202
00:49:44,850 --> 00:49:46,150
- Yes.
- That's so nice.

1203
00:49:46,320 --> 00:49:48,590
- Do you all share the same room?
- Hold on!

1204
00:49:48,590 --> 00:49:50,120
- Hold on!
- You spoke your heart right away.

1205
00:49:50,120 --> 00:49:52,160
- We were all thinking that inside.
- Right.

1206
00:49:52,460 --> 00:49:54,230
- Really?
- How did he earn this privilege?

1207
00:49:54,230 --> 00:49:55,230
Exactly.

1208
00:50:05,170 --> 00:50:07,970
(He listened to the announcement
on his own.)

1209
00:50:07,970 --> 00:50:09,340
Hold on.

1210
00:50:09,410 --> 00:50:11,880
That was why there were no prices
at the supermarket.

1211
00:50:11,880 --> 00:50:14,280
I can come up with the prices.

1212
00:50:14,480 --> 00:50:17,650
Could there be another role
other than the merchant?

1213
00:50:18,720 --> 00:50:21,320
That was the saddest part.

1214
00:50:21,320 --> 00:50:23,960
I love using the room on my own,

1215
00:50:23,960 --> 00:50:26,660
but others can chat all they want,

1216
00:50:26,660 --> 00:50:28,260
and they have someone to talk to
when feeling bored.

1217
00:50:28,260 --> 00:50:31,000
I was left alone in the room...

1218
00:50:31,000 --> 00:50:33,330
talking to myself
even during the leader selection.

1219
00:50:33,330 --> 00:50:36,500
Everything else is great,
but those parts make it hard for me.

1220
00:50:36,500 --> 00:50:37,870
I want to be on Team Black.

1221
00:50:39,770 --> 00:50:41,970
I don't know why I was given this role.

1222
00:50:42,840 --> 00:50:45,410
- You can make a lot of money.
- Right.

1223
00:50:45,410 --> 00:50:47,050
Right. I believe so.

1224
00:50:47,410 --> 00:50:48,550
- You all must make purchases to survive.
- Then,

1225
00:50:48,550 --> 00:50:49,880
the conditions must be different for him.

1226
00:50:49,880 --> 00:50:51,920
Have you been told the details?

1227
00:50:51,920 --> 00:50:54,550
I sell items to make money.

1228
00:50:54,550 --> 00:50:56,650
What?
Is this Nexus: The Kingdom of the Winds?

1229
00:50:56,650 --> 00:50:58,090
- Nexus: The Kingdom of the Winds?
- Well...

1230
00:50:58,460 --> 00:51:00,530
I know nothing. I think I'll go...

1231
00:51:00,530 --> 00:51:02,960
and check the things I can sell.

1232
00:51:02,960 --> 00:51:03,960
- Right.
- Right.

1233
00:51:03,960 --> 00:51:05,430
- Please give me a moment.
- Right.

1234
00:51:06,630 --> 00:51:09,330
This person will sell things
at the supermarket...

1235
00:51:09,330 --> 00:51:11,570
and earn diamonds? No.

1236
00:51:11,570 --> 00:51:13,300
This person knows a lot.

1237
00:51:13,300 --> 00:51:15,210
That is why I must get close to him.

1238
00:51:15,570 --> 00:51:17,040
He seems to know a lot.

1239
00:51:17,540 --> 00:51:19,980
First of all, he was really good-looking.

1240
00:51:19,980 --> 00:51:22,510
He didn't seem like someone
who would play tricks.

1241
00:51:22,510 --> 00:51:24,520
He had an image of an upright young man.

1242
00:51:24,850 --> 00:51:26,620
Vector was someone...

1243
00:51:26,620 --> 00:51:29,550
I met a few times in League of Legends.

1244
00:51:29,550 --> 00:51:32,490
We just knew each other's names.

1245
00:51:32,490 --> 00:51:35,530
But even that made me feel so relieved.

1246
00:51:35,530 --> 00:51:37,530
I had one person I knew.

1247
00:51:38,300 --> 00:51:40,370
Would you all like a cup of coffee?

1248
00:51:40,370 --> 00:51:41,900
- Are you giving these to us for free?
- Yes.

1249
00:51:41,900 --> 00:51:43,130
- Yes!
- Really?

1250
00:51:43,440 --> 00:51:45,600
Well, I'm the merchant.
I can do whatever I want.

1251
00:51:45,600 --> 00:51:47,370
- I'll take the loss.
- I see.

1252
00:51:47,640 --> 00:51:49,010
You all look tired.

1253
00:51:49,010 --> 00:51:50,010
I know what this is.

1254
00:51:50,010 --> 00:51:51,580
- Right.
- Would you like a latte?

1255
00:51:51,580 --> 00:51:53,510
- He's making us get addicted.
- Right.

1256
00:51:53,510 --> 00:51:54,710
What would you like?

1257
00:51:54,710 --> 00:51:56,050
- Can we really have these?
- Right.

1258
00:51:56,050 --> 00:51:57,620
- We won't get sick, will we?
- No.

1259
00:51:57,820 --> 00:51:59,320
We won't be charged later, will we?

1260
00:52:00,620 --> 00:52:01,620
She already drank it.

1261
00:52:01,620 --> 00:52:03,390
- Right.
- She already drank it.

1262
00:52:03,390 --> 00:52:05,960
You won't be charging us, right?

1263
00:52:05,960 --> 00:52:07,030
We have no money.

1264
00:52:07,860 --> 00:52:08,860
What?

1265
00:52:09,130 --> 00:52:10,460
- What?
- Hello.

1266
00:52:10,460 --> 00:52:12,560
- What?
- Why are there so many people?

1267
00:52:12,560 --> 00:52:14,000
- What?
- There are more people there.

1268
00:52:14,000 --> 00:52:15,930
- What?
- There are so many of them.

1269
00:52:15,930 --> 00:52:17,000
There are so many of them.

1270
00:52:17,000 --> 00:52:18,540
- Hello.
- Hello.

1271
00:52:18,540 --> 00:52:20,470
- Hello.
- Hello.

1272
00:52:20,670 --> 00:52:23,480
(All the residents finally meet
for the first time.)

1273
00:52:23,480 --> 00:52:24,480
There are seven.

1274
00:52:24,480 --> 00:52:25,480
- Hello.
- Hello.

1275
00:52:25,480 --> 00:52:27,150
- Hello.
- Hello.

1276
00:52:27,910 --> 00:52:29,050
They are in odd numbers.

1277
00:52:29,050 --> 00:52:30,050
Right.

1278
00:52:30,580 --> 00:52:33,020
A large group of six for Team White
came over all of a sudden.

1279
00:52:33,020 --> 00:52:34,590
I felt intimidated for a moment.

1280
00:52:34,590 --> 00:52:38,790
The female members
also felt quite powerful.

1281
00:52:38,790 --> 00:52:40,360
A legion came over.

1282
00:52:40,360 --> 00:52:42,160
That felt really intimidating.

1283
00:52:42,160 --> 00:52:43,960
Numbers meant a lot.

1284
00:52:43,960 --> 00:52:46,630
They went,
"Why are there so many of them?"

1285
00:52:46,630 --> 00:52:48,430
That is actually our weakness.

1286
00:52:48,430 --> 00:52:50,340
That means we have to spend more
of our resources.

1287
00:52:50,340 --> 00:52:52,800
There were six of us.

1288
00:52:52,800 --> 00:52:55,340
There's a sense of mediocrity
that can be felt...

1289
00:52:55,340 --> 00:52:56,710
from a large number.

1290
00:52:56,710 --> 00:52:59,680
"Are we the leftovers?"

1291
00:52:59,680 --> 00:53:01,410
I felt slightly annoyed
while thinking that.

1292
00:53:01,980 --> 00:53:03,250
Genie.

1293
00:53:03,250 --> 00:53:04,280
Why is she here?

1294
00:53:04,280 --> 00:53:05,520
Why has she come again?

1295
00:53:05,520 --> 00:53:09,250
The one in Team Red
was someone in the accounting field.

1296
00:53:09,250 --> 00:53:11,560
I thought that things would feel
much different for them.

1297
00:53:11,560 --> 00:53:12,720
There were many mouths to feed.

1298
00:53:12,720 --> 00:53:15,960
But the many members
could be used to their advantage.

1299
00:53:15,960 --> 00:53:17,800
They all gave off good first impressions.

1300
00:53:17,800 --> 00:53:20,470
The members of Team Red
had the worst first impressions.

1301
00:53:21,870 --> 00:53:24,740
For some reason,
it feels like you were friends like this,

1302
00:53:24,740 --> 00:53:26,100
- and you were friends like this.
- Based on their images.

1303
00:53:26,100 --> 00:53:28,110
- Did the members of Team White...
- They all look like...

1304
00:53:28,110 --> 00:53:29,270
all know each other?

1305
00:53:29,270 --> 00:53:31,480
No. It's our first time seeing each other.

1306
00:53:31,710 --> 00:53:34,350
It feels like
an intimidatingly large family.

1307
00:53:34,350 --> 00:53:35,710
- Right.
- Right.

1308
00:53:35,710 --> 00:53:37,850
- It feels like we're short on numbers.
- Right.

1309
00:53:37,850 --> 00:53:38,850
They all look close.

1310
00:53:38,850 --> 00:53:40,690
But if you all think about it,

1311
00:53:40,690 --> 00:53:42,850
season one was about
ideology verification.

1312
00:53:42,850 --> 00:53:45,960
- Right.
- We all know Genie's ideology.

1313
00:53:46,390 --> 00:53:48,590
- So, that's not our goal for this season.
- Right.

1314
00:53:48,590 --> 00:53:50,300
If so, I have to leave today.

1315
00:53:52,060 --> 00:53:53,900
- It's not verification.
- It's not verification.

1316
00:53:53,900 --> 00:53:55,400
I think it would be great to...

1317
00:53:55,400 --> 00:53:57,570
- think about the purpose of this season.
- Right.

1318
00:54:02,010 --> 00:54:04,440
Residents who have moved into
Community Town,

1319
00:54:04,810 --> 00:54:05,810
welcome.

1320
00:54:06,010 --> 00:54:08,880
I will explain the rules...

1321
00:54:08,880 --> 00:54:11,220
for your 9 days and 8 nights
of stay in Community Town.

1322
00:54:11,580 --> 00:54:15,020
Community Town consists of several groups.

1323
00:54:15,920 --> 00:54:19,020
The members of each group
of different colors live together.

1324
00:54:19,490 --> 00:54:22,330
The dorm of another group
can not be entered.

1325
00:54:23,430 --> 00:54:26,830
All information,
including resource status,

1326
00:54:26,830 --> 00:54:28,970
may not be shared...

1327
00:54:28,970 --> 00:54:31,000
and must be kept confidential
from each group.

1328
00:54:31,370 --> 00:54:35,070
The schedule of 9 days and 8 nights
will operate like this.

1329
00:54:35,610 --> 00:54:37,810
Productive activities will take place
in the morning.

1330
00:54:37,810 --> 00:54:40,510
Trade activities will take place
in the afternoon.

1331
00:54:40,510 --> 00:54:43,110
Every dinner must be eaten...

1332
00:54:43,110 --> 00:54:45,480
with all groups gathered in the Town Hall.

1333
00:54:45,480 --> 00:54:47,090
- All groups gathered?
- There's a time for trading.

1334
00:54:47,090 --> 00:54:50,220
Each group has a specialized skill.

1335
00:54:50,790 --> 00:54:53,360
Team White can decide the prize money.

1336
00:54:53,860 --> 00:54:56,290
The diamonds that are used as a standard
of prize money distribution...

1337
00:54:56,290 --> 00:54:59,400
can only be issued by Team White.

1338
00:54:59,800 --> 00:55:01,870
After trading with Team White,

1339
00:55:01,870 --> 00:55:03,470
secure your prize money.

1340
00:55:03,470 --> 00:55:05,100
Gosh, that's really important.

1341
00:55:05,100 --> 00:55:07,140
- That's why the colors are like that.
- Right.

1342
00:55:07,640 --> 00:55:10,640
- Team Blue decides survival.
- That was what the colors meant.

1343
00:55:10,640 --> 00:55:13,480
The absolute majority of lives required
for survival...

1344
00:55:13,480 --> 00:55:15,710
can be produced by Team Blue.

1345
00:55:16,480 --> 00:55:17,580
In order to avoid elimination...

1346
00:55:17,580 --> 00:55:19,280
- and survive until the end,
- Let's go!

1347
00:55:19,580 --> 00:55:22,790
- make good deals with Team Blue.
- Good.

1348
00:55:22,790 --> 00:55:24,160
- That's great.
- That's great.

1349
00:55:24,960 --> 00:55:25,960
That's important.

1350
00:55:26,520 --> 00:55:29,060
Team Red controls the game.

1351
00:55:29,630 --> 00:55:32,030
Credits are required for all activities.

1352
00:55:32,560 --> 00:55:36,870
Team Red has an overwhelming efficiency
when it comes to credit production.

1353
00:55:37,600 --> 00:55:39,640
Without Team Red's credits,

1354
00:55:39,640 --> 00:55:42,040
all activities will stop.

1355
00:55:43,840 --> 00:55:45,480
That must stop
all profit-making activities.

1356
00:55:45,910 --> 00:55:48,250
Only Team Black...

1357
00:55:48,250 --> 00:55:51,220
will be able to check
the resource information.

1358
00:55:51,780 --> 00:55:55,490
Use Team Black
to check each other's information.

1359
00:55:55,850 --> 00:56:00,730
However, Team Black is not required
to share the information accurately.

1360
00:56:01,460 --> 00:56:03,960
Throughout the whole 9 days and 8 nights,

1361
00:56:03,960 --> 00:56:08,800
all groceries may only be purchased
at Team Black's supermarket.

1362
00:56:08,800 --> 00:56:09,800
That must be so exciting.

1363
00:56:09,800 --> 00:56:13,340
To run the supermarket,
one life is required for one day.

1364
00:56:13,340 --> 00:56:14,770
As today is the first day,

1365
00:56:14,770 --> 00:56:17,810
the supermarket can be used
without a life.

1366
00:56:17,810 --> 00:56:19,280
- That's a relief.
- That would've been terrible.

1367
00:56:19,480 --> 00:56:22,010
So each group will produce
their respective resources in the morning.

1368
00:56:22,010 --> 00:56:24,050
- Right.
- All of us must work hard...

1369
00:56:24,050 --> 00:56:25,720
for us to prosper.

1370
00:56:25,720 --> 00:56:26,720
I believe so.

1371
00:56:27,120 --> 00:56:29,420
I thought it was well-balanced.

1372
00:56:29,420 --> 00:56:32,420
All three resources were connected,

1373
00:56:32,420 --> 00:56:34,390
and not even one of them
could be left out.

1374
00:56:34,390 --> 00:56:36,290
All three resources were necessary.

1375
00:56:36,290 --> 00:56:39,200
All of them played a part.

1376
00:56:39,200 --> 00:56:40,900
The dominant strategy of this situation...

1377
00:56:40,900 --> 00:56:45,170
is to persuade the others that
our resource is the most valuable.

1378
00:56:45,170 --> 00:56:48,210
Economically, it's the Invisible Hand.

1379
00:56:48,210 --> 00:56:49,870
The Invisible Hand...

1380
00:56:49,870 --> 00:56:51,840
represents everyone's greed
gathered together,

1381
00:56:51,840 --> 00:56:54,650
forming reasonable market prices.

1382
00:56:54,650 --> 00:56:58,150
A beneficial result for everyone...

1383
00:56:58,150 --> 00:56:59,980
is the outcome of this.

1384
00:57:02,690 --> 00:57:06,820
The current total amount of resources
of Community Town will be revealed.

1385
00:57:07,390 --> 00:57:11,630
After this announcement,
there will be no more announcements.

1386
00:57:11,630 --> 00:57:14,530
(Total Resources)

1387
00:57:14,530 --> 00:57:20,300
So there's a total of 43 lives.

1388
00:57:20,300 --> 00:57:21,540
- What? That's not right.
- We have 43?

1389
00:57:21,540 --> 00:57:23,910
- That's the total number.
- That's the total number.

1390
00:57:23,910 --> 00:57:26,640
Don't they have all of them?

1391
00:57:26,640 --> 00:57:27,750
(Team Blue Lives: 30)

1392
00:57:28,050 --> 00:57:29,580
- But...
- In our room,

1393
00:57:29,580 --> 00:57:30,820
there was a display of our lives...

1394
00:57:30,820 --> 00:57:32,350
- and our credits.
- Those are excluded?

1395
00:57:32,350 --> 00:57:34,690
Each group has its
own number of resources.

1396
00:57:34,690 --> 00:57:36,250
All groups must reveal their numbers...

1397
00:57:36,250 --> 00:57:38,560
- for us to know.
- Right.

1398
00:57:38,560 --> 00:57:40,190
- But they can't be revealed.
- Aren't we supposed not to?

1399
00:57:40,190 --> 00:57:42,430
- That will put you at a disadvantage.
- Right.

1400
00:57:42,430 --> 00:57:45,060
Good deals must be made with each other.

1401
00:57:45,860 --> 00:57:47,830
This is a funny situation.

1402
00:57:48,270 --> 00:57:50,130
That's including Team Black, right?

1403
00:57:50,130 --> 00:57:52,640
Yes. I believe that includes my resources.

1404
00:57:53,870 --> 00:57:55,270
(Team Black's Resources:
0 diamonds, 1 life, 0 credits)

1405
00:57:55,270 --> 00:57:58,680
I only had one life.

1406
00:57:58,680 --> 00:58:00,340
I was already the only one left
in my team,

1407
00:58:00,340 --> 00:58:03,210
so lives were meaningless to me
when it comes to survival.

1408
00:58:03,210 --> 00:58:06,820
Does that mean the lives are only useful
for me to open the store?

1409
00:58:06,820 --> 00:58:07,990
I opened the rule book,

1410
00:58:07,990 --> 00:58:11,820
and it said that Team Black could not
make deals regarding lives or credits.

1411
00:58:11,820 --> 00:58:15,330
"Will I have to close down
after opening the store tomorrow?"

1412
00:58:15,330 --> 00:58:17,130
I was filled with all kinds of thoughts.

1413
00:58:17,730 --> 00:58:21,230
I didn't think the resources
would be this imbalanced.

1414
00:58:21,230 --> 00:58:24,340
We had 30 of 36 credits.

1415
00:58:24,340 --> 00:58:27,670
Since Team Black cannot make deals
with credits,

1416
00:58:27,670 --> 00:58:31,910
if I infer that the other teams
were given three,

1417
00:58:31,910 --> 00:58:33,240
that's ten times more for our team.

1418
00:58:33,240 --> 00:58:37,080
That made me think that
we could take a dominant position.

1419
00:58:37,080 --> 00:58:40,180
Each team was stacked
with their respective resource.

1420
00:58:40,180 --> 00:58:43,350
The members of Team White
were short on lives.

1421
00:58:43,350 --> 00:58:46,160
They must be the ones
who feel the most anxious.

1422
00:58:46,160 --> 00:58:49,560
Team Red had enough
to survive for two days,

1423
00:58:49,560 --> 00:58:53,970
but it seemed like we had
the most power at this point.

1424
00:58:53,970 --> 00:58:56,800
But the other teams were given...

1425
00:58:56,800 --> 00:58:58,740
small amounts of credits and lives.

1426
00:58:58,740 --> 00:59:00,340
Why didn't we get a small amount
of diamonds?

1427
00:59:00,340 --> 00:59:02,340
We should've been given diamonds.

1428
00:59:02,570 --> 00:59:05,540
The trading for Day 1 will begin.

1429
00:59:06,410 --> 00:59:10,650
Each group may freely
negotiate for three hours.

1430
00:59:11,020 --> 00:59:14,320
When a deal is made,
the leaders of the groups...

1431
00:59:14,590 --> 00:59:17,360
must bring the resources from the dorms...

1432
00:59:17,360 --> 00:59:21,530
and visit the Community Center
with their trade partners.

1433
00:59:21,560 --> 00:59:22,560
(Occupied: Please wait.)

1434
00:59:22,560 --> 00:59:25,460
Two groups can make a trade at once.

1435
00:59:25,460 --> 00:59:28,700
When one credit
is put into the credit box,

1436
00:59:28,700 --> 00:59:30,800
the Trading Station will be activated.

1437
00:59:31,570 --> 00:59:35,110
When the resources are inserted
into the Trading Station,

1438
00:59:35,110 --> 00:59:36,740
the deal begins.

1439
00:59:37,480 --> 00:59:40,010
Black is the operator of the supermarket.

1440
00:59:40,010 --> 00:59:43,450
Please enter the supermarket
and start your business.

1441
00:59:44,250 --> 00:59:47,850
While wishing everyone good deals,

1442
00:59:47,850 --> 00:59:51,320
this announcement
at the Community Center will now end.

1443
00:59:51,490 --> 00:59:52,560
- I see.
- Right.

1444
00:59:53,720 --> 00:59:56,060
The theme is Invisible Hand.

1445
00:59:56,060 --> 00:59:59,630
The trade system
is at the center of everything.

1446
00:59:59,630 --> 01:00:01,430
We must understand which resource
has a high scarcity value...

1447
01:00:01,430 --> 01:00:03,400
and how to set the exchange rate.

1448
01:00:03,400 --> 01:00:07,710
I've studied a bit more on economics
than the others.

1449
01:00:07,710 --> 01:00:10,440
I believe I have a better chance...

1450
01:00:10,440 --> 01:00:13,510
of understanding the economic designs.

1451
01:00:13,510 --> 01:00:17,050
All groups have an advantage
in different resources.

1452
01:00:17,050 --> 01:00:18,650
All three resources are needed.

1453
01:00:18,650 --> 01:00:21,650
The key point is to maintain
our team's advantage...

1454
01:00:21,650 --> 01:00:24,560
while sneaking in
the resources of the other teams.

1455
01:00:24,560 --> 01:00:28,290
It felt like it was going to be
a headache to go against each other.

1456
01:00:28,290 --> 01:00:30,760
We were all split into teams.

1457
01:00:30,760 --> 01:00:33,100
"You only have this. You only have that."

1458
01:00:33,100 --> 01:00:34,730
"You must compete against each other."

1459
01:00:34,730 --> 01:00:36,670
As it was designed this way,

1460
01:00:36,670 --> 01:00:39,640
I thought that things could get
quite cruel from the start.

1461
01:00:39,640 --> 01:00:42,640
We are about to enter a gruesome fight.

1462
01:00:43,110 --> 01:00:45,540
We have credits,

1463
01:00:45,540 --> 01:00:49,210
but we need lives and diamonds.

1464
01:00:49,410 --> 01:00:50,850
- Right.
- Right.

1465
01:00:50,850 --> 01:00:51,980
- That must be the same for everyone.
- So...

1466
01:00:51,980 --> 01:00:53,380
- The ones who have a lot of diamonds...
- Right.

1467
01:00:53,380 --> 01:00:54,850
Don't you all need resources?

1468
01:00:54,850 --> 01:00:57,820
- We do.
- Could we have some time...

1469
01:00:57,820 --> 01:01:00,020
- to have a group meeting?
- Right. We need that.

1470
01:01:00,020 --> 01:01:02,030
- Shall we go?
- Yes.

1471
01:01:02,030 --> 01:01:03,160
- Sounds good.
- Right.

1472
01:01:03,160 --> 01:01:04,730
I'll open the store first.

1473
01:01:04,730 --> 01:01:06,200
- All right.
- Let's move out...

1474
01:01:06,200 --> 01:01:08,170
- with our groups.
- Right.

1475
01:01:09,200 --> 01:01:10,200
- Gosh.
- But...

1476
01:01:10,200 --> 01:01:11,440
- Are we going to sit here?
- Yes.

1477
01:01:11,440 --> 01:01:12,700
- I'm glad I didn't become the leader.
- Great.

1478
01:01:14,010 --> 01:01:16,570
(Black opens the supermarket.)

1479
01:01:24,720 --> 01:01:28,490
(Black can track each group's
resource information in real time.)

1480
01:01:29,420 --> 01:01:31,760
The numbers for the first day appeared.

1481
01:01:31,760 --> 01:01:33,860
And there was that announcement.
"After today's announcement,"

1482
01:01:33,860 --> 01:01:35,930
"the total amount will not be revealed."

1483
01:01:35,930 --> 01:01:36,930
That got me excited.

1484
01:01:36,930 --> 01:01:41,270
Everyone knows the number of resources
each group has at the start,

1485
01:01:41,270 --> 01:01:42,570
but as time goes by,

1486
01:01:42,570 --> 01:01:45,170
the information asymmetry
will grow bigger.

1487
01:01:45,170 --> 01:01:47,570
That will make more people
need Black's help.

1488
01:01:48,470 --> 01:01:50,570
We can issue diamonds on the second day.

1489
01:01:50,570 --> 01:01:52,880
The total amount is 200.

1490
01:01:52,880 --> 01:01:55,550
If we issue more diamonds,
the value of the diamonds will drop...

1491
01:01:55,550 --> 01:01:57,680
- if the prize money doesn't get bigger.
- The value will drop.

1492
01:01:57,680 --> 01:01:59,920
Group White can make decisions
regarding the prize money.

1493
01:01:59,920 --> 01:02:03,450
This group can issue more diamonds
starting from the second day.

1494
01:02:03,750 --> 01:02:07,260
However, the total prize money
of 200,000 dollars will not change.

1495
01:02:07,260 --> 01:02:11,530
As more diamonds are issued,
the value of one diamond will drop.

1496
01:02:12,200 --> 01:02:15,570
For example, if there is just one diamond,

1497
01:02:15,570 --> 01:02:19,140
it will be worth 200,000 dollars.

1498
01:02:19,640 --> 01:02:21,270
But if there are two diamonds,

1499
01:02:21,270 --> 01:02:24,840
the value of 1 diamond
will become 100,000 dollars.

1500
01:02:25,780 --> 01:02:28,450
If Team White makes a deal...

1501
01:02:28,450 --> 01:02:33,020
to give away 100 diamonds
that are worth 100,000 dollars,

1502
01:02:33,020 --> 01:02:36,050
and 200 diamonds are issued the next day,

1503
01:02:36,520 --> 01:02:41,190
the other group's prize money
could drop to 50,000 dollars.

1504
01:02:42,230 --> 01:02:45,760
The number of how many we can issue
and the value drop of each diamond...

1505
01:02:45,760 --> 01:02:47,360
is only given to us.

1506
01:02:47,360 --> 01:02:49,500
The other teams don't know
that we have 8 lives and 3 credits.

1507
01:02:49,500 --> 01:02:50,530
- They don't.
- They don't.

1508
01:02:50,530 --> 01:02:51,940
- They might think we have none.
- Right.

1509
01:02:51,940 --> 01:02:55,040
It could be, "Give us 100 diamonds
for 6 lives."

1510
01:02:55,040 --> 01:02:57,480
It will be important
to hide this information.

1511
01:02:57,480 --> 01:03:01,280
If we are to survive for two days,

1512
01:03:01,280 --> 01:03:03,510
we need four more lives.

1513
01:03:04,420 --> 01:03:06,420
I think we should ask for 16 lives.

1514
01:03:08,050 --> 01:03:09,990
I think this is what we can say.

1515
01:03:09,990 --> 01:03:12,490
- "Let's all survive for four days."
- Right.

1516
01:03:12,490 --> 01:03:14,760
- The other groups have three members.
- Right.

1517
01:03:15,290 --> 01:03:16,660
They need at least 12 lives.

1518
01:03:17,600 --> 01:03:18,600
They need 12 lives.

1519
01:03:18,600 --> 01:03:20,560
- To survive for four days.
- Right.

1520
01:03:20,900 --> 01:03:23,470
This is the total number of lives,
but they don't know how many we have.

1521
01:03:23,930 --> 01:03:26,170
This many are left after 24 lives.

1522
01:03:26,170 --> 01:03:27,570
(Remaining lives: 19)

1523
01:03:27,570 --> 01:03:29,810
So it won't matter if we ask for 16.

1524
01:03:29,810 --> 01:03:31,680
(Remaining lives: 3)

1525
01:03:31,880 --> 01:03:35,680
We can claim that we have fewer lives
compared to our six members.

1526
01:03:35,680 --> 01:03:37,310
- Shouldn't we go for that claim?
- Like that?

1527
01:03:37,310 --> 01:03:40,790
While keeping the line of comfort,
is this the biggest number we can ask for?

1528
01:03:40,790 --> 01:03:43,720
We'll say that 16 will be worth
3 days for us.

1529
01:03:43,720 --> 01:03:44,820
"We have six members."

1530
01:03:44,820 --> 01:03:46,060
"Your group can survive
for more than four days."

1531
01:03:46,060 --> 01:03:47,220
"We only have three days."

1532
01:03:47,220 --> 01:03:48,430
"Since we are at a disadvantage,"

1533
01:03:48,430 --> 01:03:50,660
"we should be given this much."
This is what we're aiming for.

1534
01:03:50,660 --> 01:03:51,660
Right.

1535
01:03:52,160 --> 01:03:54,060
I don't think three days' worth of lives
will be given to us.

1536
01:03:54,060 --> 01:03:56,300
We'll have to ask
how many they are willing to offer.

1537
01:03:56,300 --> 01:03:58,270
Pretend to faint
if they are willing to offer just one.

1538
01:03:59,200 --> 01:04:00,810
- "We're going to die."
- Well...

1539
01:04:00,810 --> 01:04:01,910
Well,

1540
01:04:02,040 --> 01:04:04,880
they could offer six lives
so we can survive for a single day.

1541
01:04:04,880 --> 01:04:06,140
- Right. They could do that.
- Isn't that too harsh?

1542
01:04:06,140 --> 01:04:07,680
Plead for their understanding.

1543
01:04:07,680 --> 01:04:09,250
- I will. I will, but...
- You should faint.

1544
01:04:09,510 --> 01:04:11,580
I want to collect
as many lives as possible.

1545
01:04:11,580 --> 01:04:14,350
I want to collect enough lives...

1546
01:04:14,350 --> 01:04:16,350
for us to survive for three days.

1547
01:04:16,350 --> 01:04:17,860
We were just given money.

1548
01:04:17,860 --> 01:04:19,260
(We were just given money.)

1549
01:04:19,260 --> 01:04:20,790
We might end up dying in a couple of days.

1550
01:04:20,790 --> 01:04:22,690
The other teams...

1551
01:04:22,690 --> 01:04:25,030
aren't in desperate need of money.

1552
01:04:25,030 --> 01:04:28,570
They will try to survive
until the very end.

1553
01:04:28,570 --> 01:04:30,970
That means a lot of money
will have to be used.

1554
01:04:32,670 --> 01:04:34,410
This was on the screen earlier.

1555
01:04:34,410 --> 01:04:36,310
- In our room?
- Right. In our room.

1556
01:04:36,310 --> 01:04:38,410
- This was on the screen.
- Right. This was on the screen.

1557
01:04:38,410 --> 01:04:40,710
If we have 30 lives...

1558
01:04:40,710 --> 01:04:43,250
Each group might have 6 lives
while Team Black has 1.

1559
01:04:43,480 --> 01:04:46,180
- Black has one life as well?
- Right. With that as an assumption.

1560
01:04:46,180 --> 01:04:47,350
Then...

1561
01:04:47,350 --> 01:04:49,990
he will have none after today.

1562
01:04:50,350 --> 01:04:51,560
- Right.
- Right.

1563
01:04:51,560 --> 01:04:55,360
By the end of tomorrow,
he will be removed if he has no lives.

1564
01:04:55,360 --> 01:04:59,100
Actually,
we don't have to do anything today.

1565
01:04:59,300 --> 01:05:00,830
- There's nothing to worry about.
- After paying up the credits,

1566
01:05:00,830 --> 01:05:03,170
- we can get more tomorrow.
- Right.

1567
01:05:03,170 --> 01:05:05,100
- Each group...
- Why don't we give up on the prize money?

1568
01:05:05,100 --> 01:05:07,470
Should we send them all away
and have a good time between ourselves?

1569
01:05:07,470 --> 01:05:09,240
That's still a win for us.

1570
01:05:09,240 --> 01:05:11,110
- That's a huge win.
- Just the three of us.

1571
01:05:11,110 --> 01:05:12,140
Should we hang around until the end?

1572
01:05:12,140 --> 01:05:14,410
They're urgent right now...

1573
01:05:14,410 --> 01:05:16,410
- Right.
- based on this assumption.

1574
01:05:16,410 --> 01:05:19,080
I was so happy
that the lives were given to us.

1575
01:05:19,080 --> 01:05:22,220
"I won't have to worry about
survival for now."

1576
01:05:22,220 --> 01:05:24,290
Lives are the most crucial.

1577
01:05:24,290 --> 01:05:26,960
No matter how many diamonds
and credits they have,

1578
01:05:26,960 --> 01:05:28,990
they will all be meaningless
without any lives.

1579
01:05:29,160 --> 01:05:32,430
This can be used as a threat...

1580
01:05:32,660 --> 01:05:34,370
to drain out everything they have.

1581
01:05:34,370 --> 01:05:36,070
"Hand them over if you wish to live."

1582
01:05:36,830 --> 01:05:39,440
Our leader is a tank of ambition.

1583
01:05:39,740 --> 01:05:44,140
When dogs win competitions abroad,

1584
01:05:44,140 --> 01:05:45,880
the dogs look really proud.

1585
01:05:45,980 --> 01:05:48,510
She's just like a champion dog.

1586
01:05:48,510 --> 01:05:51,280
It's quite awkward
to compare her to a dog,

1587
01:05:51,280 --> 01:05:55,020
but I felt this great sense of confidence.

1588
01:05:55,750 --> 01:05:56,850
Things aren't bad for now.

1589
01:05:56,850 --> 01:05:58,120
(Team Red's Resources:
0 diamonds, 4 lives, 30 credits)

1590
01:05:58,120 --> 01:06:00,290
- I get the idea.
- Right.

1591
01:06:00,620 --> 01:06:02,630
We are the only ones...

1592
01:06:03,060 --> 01:06:04,060
with credits.

1593
01:06:04,060 --> 01:06:05,100
- Right.
- Right.

1594
01:06:05,100 --> 01:06:07,560
Then, the lowest resource...

1595
01:06:07,560 --> 01:06:10,170
has the highest value.

1596
01:06:10,170 --> 01:06:12,870
The problem with the credits...

1597
01:06:12,870 --> 01:06:14,240
is that we only know
they are needed for deals.

1598
01:06:14,240 --> 01:06:15,940
We don't know where else they are needed.

1599
01:06:15,940 --> 01:06:18,810
- Right.
- The importance of credits...

1600
01:06:18,810 --> 01:06:20,280
is lower than lives.

1601
01:06:20,280 --> 01:06:22,950
Since we have 30 credits,

1602
01:06:22,950 --> 01:06:25,850
if a trade occurs today
and they lose their credits,

1603
01:06:25,850 --> 01:06:27,820
- they will have no credits tomorrow.
- Right.

1604
01:06:27,820 --> 01:06:29,790
- We won't be able to buy lives.
- Right.

1605
01:06:30,020 --> 01:06:32,760
We must make a deal today to play it safe.

1606
01:06:32,760 --> 01:06:34,960
But the value of credits
might go up tomorrow.

1607
01:06:34,960 --> 01:06:37,190
How many lives shall we target?

1608
01:06:37,190 --> 01:06:38,330
Five.

1609
01:06:38,330 --> 01:06:39,330
We'll just make a big call?

1610
01:06:39,330 --> 01:06:41,600
We need enough to survive for three days.

1611
01:06:41,600 --> 01:06:45,040
- We should ask for five from Team Blue.
- Right.

1612
01:06:45,040 --> 01:06:47,740
I want to meet up with Black next.

1613
01:06:47,740 --> 01:06:49,810
I want to ask him if Team White
has the most diamonds.

1614
01:06:50,870 --> 01:06:52,640
And as for diamonds,

1615
01:06:52,640 --> 01:06:55,080
- we'll go for the minimum.
- We need them.

1616
01:06:56,050 --> 01:06:58,180
We can ask for 20 diamonds for 2 credits.

1617
01:06:58,880 --> 01:07:01,090
I don't want to give away
too many credits.

1618
01:07:01,090 --> 01:07:02,090
- Right.
- Right.

1619
01:07:02,290 --> 01:07:04,990
(Maillard wants to raise the value
by limiting the distribution.)

1620
01:07:09,230 --> 01:07:10,460
- Yes?
- Do you have some time?

1621
01:07:10,760 --> 01:07:12,200
Yes. Give me a moment.

1622
01:07:12,300 --> 01:07:13,800
- Come to us if you have time.
- Right.

1623
01:07:14,030 --> 01:07:16,230
- We can give up on the diamonds.
- Right.

1624
01:07:16,230 --> 01:07:18,470
(Team Red and Team Black meet up.)

1625
01:07:18,470 --> 01:07:19,570
- Right now...
- I was working on the stock.

1626
01:07:19,570 --> 01:07:21,470
- Oh, right. You must feel lonely.
- Right now,

1627
01:07:21,740 --> 01:07:23,010
- according to the rules,
- Yes?

1628
01:07:23,010 --> 01:07:25,240
you can't make a deal
with lives or credits.

1629
01:07:25,240 --> 01:07:28,080
I can't. I'm someone
who doesn't need the credits.

1630
01:07:28,350 --> 01:07:30,180
What I could gain from Team Red is...

1631
01:07:30,180 --> 01:07:31,850
- Diamonds?
- something else using the credits.

1632
01:07:31,850 --> 01:07:35,420
Are diamonds what you want the most
during the remaining time?

1633
01:07:35,420 --> 01:07:37,220
Yes, the diamonds. The prize.

1634
01:07:37,320 --> 01:07:39,190
- Right, the prize. I get it.
- Yes.

1635
01:07:39,190 --> 01:07:42,230
From what I see,
credits are important in the early stage.

1636
01:07:42,230 --> 01:07:44,400
- Totally.
- Since you have the upper hand,

1637
01:07:44,400 --> 01:07:47,100
getting as many diamonds as possible
early on seems like a good idea.

1638
01:07:47,100 --> 01:07:49,370
- I'll be frank with you.
- Okay.

1639
01:07:49,370 --> 01:07:50,600
I have zero diamonds.

1640
01:07:50,870 --> 01:07:52,770
- The other team has it all.
- Right.

1641
01:07:52,770 --> 01:07:54,940
Get as many diamonds as possible
from Team White.

1642
01:07:54,940 --> 01:07:57,470
When tomorrow and the day after come...

1643
01:07:57,470 --> 01:07:58,510
Become the best customers?

1644
01:07:58,510 --> 01:08:00,140
Yes, it'd be best if you do.

1645
01:08:00,140 --> 01:08:03,750
I wanted to seem like
a very transparent person,

1646
01:08:03,750 --> 01:08:06,150
and I was actually being transparent.

1647
01:08:06,150 --> 01:08:08,790
Using the power of the information I have,

1648
01:08:08,790 --> 01:08:13,190
I thought I'd be able to control them
from a favorable position.

1649
01:08:13,290 --> 01:08:17,330
Black has a personal goal,

1650
01:08:17,330 --> 01:08:21,400
and to achieve it, he must think that
it's in his best interest...

1651
01:08:21,400 --> 01:08:24,870
to build as much goodwill as possible.

1652
01:08:24,870 --> 01:08:26,800
After being nice to us,

1653
01:08:26,800 --> 01:08:28,510
I'm sure he'd want to stab us in the back.

1654
01:08:28,510 --> 01:08:30,970
But whose back will he stab?

1655
01:08:30,970 --> 01:08:33,440
Everyone's or a team's?

1656
01:08:34,080 --> 01:08:35,450
Here's a tip.

1657
01:08:35,580 --> 01:08:37,410
Use the lives to threaten others.

1658
01:08:37,410 --> 01:08:39,050
- I had the same idea.
- Right?

1659
01:08:39,120 --> 01:08:42,150
The value of life
will be the highest today.

1660
01:08:42,420 --> 01:08:45,020
You don't have any diamonds
at the moment, right?

1661
01:08:45,020 --> 01:08:46,490
- Right.
- I recommend securing...

1662
01:08:46,490 --> 01:08:47,830
as many diamonds as possible...

1663
01:08:47,830 --> 01:08:49,430
when there's an information gap.

1664
01:08:49,690 --> 01:08:52,100
I don't think it's wise to buy
many diamonds right now.

1665
01:08:52,100 --> 01:08:55,300
Earlier, Black said buying more
would be beneficial.

1666
01:08:55,430 --> 01:08:57,070
- It's currently 1,000 dollars.
- Right.

1667
01:08:57,070 --> 01:08:58,600
- But once more are produced,
- It could drop to 500 dollars.

1668
01:08:58,600 --> 01:08:59,670
say 500 diamonds,

1669
01:08:59,670 --> 01:09:01,270
- it'll plunge to 400 dollars per diamond.
- Right.

1670
01:09:01,610 --> 01:09:03,710
- The value will drop by more than half.
- True.

1671
01:09:03,710 --> 01:09:07,480
But if submitting one life per day
is a fixed requirement,

1672
01:09:07,710 --> 01:09:10,180
then the value of both lives and credits
will remain stable...

1673
01:09:10,180 --> 01:09:12,150
- because funds are constantly spent.
- Right.

1674
01:09:12,580 --> 01:09:14,020
Black is here.

1675
01:09:14,020 --> 01:09:15,850
- Give him a round of applause first.
- Welcome.

1676
01:09:15,850 --> 01:09:17,120
(Team White and Team Black
meet face to face.)

1677
01:09:17,450 --> 01:09:19,420
- Hi.
- Take a seat, Black.

1678
01:09:21,130 --> 01:09:22,830
What's going on right now?

1679
01:09:23,090 --> 01:09:26,230
I'm sharing my thoughts objectively today,

1680
01:09:26,230 --> 01:09:28,330
- without any personal interests involved.
- I see.

1681
01:09:28,330 --> 01:09:30,500
Nobody shared how much money they had,

1682
01:09:30,500 --> 01:09:32,300
but I expressed that
lives would be crucial early on,

1683
01:09:32,470 --> 01:09:34,970
while diamonds would matter more
in the second half.

1684
01:09:35,470 --> 01:09:38,240
The prices can be manipulated as desired.

1685
01:09:38,780 --> 01:09:41,680
Say that it was 200 today
but that becomes 2,000.

1686
01:09:42,350 --> 01:09:44,920
The price would suddenly drop
from 1,000 dollars to 100 dollars.

1687
01:09:44,920 --> 01:09:46,180
- Right.
- But they wouldn't know that.

1688
01:09:46,420 --> 01:09:48,590
Even if you must use many diamonds,

1689
01:09:48,590 --> 01:09:51,820
I think it's best to secure
many lives and credits.

1690
01:09:51,820 --> 01:09:54,460
But the other teams
would try to sell the least...

1691
01:09:54,460 --> 01:09:55,560
at a high price.

1692
01:09:56,160 --> 01:10:00,030
Here's what I can offer
to support Team White.

1693
01:10:00,030 --> 01:10:02,200
For example, I could suddenly raise
the prices tomorrow.

1694
01:10:02,200 --> 01:10:05,840
I can make it so that
no one except Team White can eat.

1695
01:10:05,840 --> 01:10:07,140
I bet they'd just starve.

1696
01:10:08,070 --> 01:10:09,140
Will they?

1697
01:10:10,110 --> 01:10:11,680
- Won't they?
- You see,

1698
01:10:11,680 --> 01:10:13,080
we're kind people.

1699
01:10:13,380 --> 01:10:14,980
- What?
- That's true.

1700
01:10:14,980 --> 01:10:17,150
- Do you know why we're Team White?
- We're Team White because...

1701
01:10:17,210 --> 01:10:20,080
we want everyone to survive...

1702
01:10:20,080 --> 01:10:21,190
- and strive together.
- If I'm being honest,

1703
01:10:21,190 --> 01:10:23,220
the more people survive,

1704
01:10:23,220 --> 01:10:25,460
- the more we can produce,
- True.

1705
01:10:25,620 --> 01:10:27,520
and that means I can mine more diamonds.

1706
01:10:27,690 --> 01:10:29,490
My sole aim is the diamonds.

1707
01:10:30,160 --> 01:10:31,560
When you've earned plenty,

1708
01:10:31,860 --> 01:10:33,160
you don't have to open the shop.

1709
01:10:33,160 --> 01:10:34,160
- Will he leave us to starve?
- The thing is,

1710
01:10:34,160 --> 01:10:36,300
even if I think I've earned enough,

1711
01:10:36,300 --> 01:10:39,540
- We could do something about it.
- Team White might suddenly print money.

1712
01:10:39,540 --> 01:10:41,540
That's why I must get on your good side.

1713
01:10:41,540 --> 01:10:43,240
True, and we must get on yours.

1714
01:10:43,240 --> 01:10:45,540
Exactly.
I'd appreciate it if you adored me.

1715
01:10:45,880 --> 01:10:47,010
- Please.
- And you're all alone.

1716
01:10:47,080 --> 01:10:48,250
You're adorable.

1717
01:10:51,450 --> 01:10:55,090
My only aim is the diamonds.

1718
01:10:55,090 --> 01:10:56,420
- White is the only team...
- I see.

1719
01:10:56,420 --> 01:10:57,490
I can coexist with.

1720
01:10:57,890 --> 01:11:00,160
"What is he trying to do?"

1721
01:11:00,390 --> 01:11:01,490
"And right there at that."

1722
01:11:01,490 --> 01:11:03,730
He's not taking part in the trade.

1723
01:11:03,730 --> 01:11:06,060
In short, he's an outsider.

1724
01:11:06,060 --> 01:11:09,430
He'll definitely make moves
according to rules different from ours.

1725
01:11:09,430 --> 01:11:11,970
That left me with no choice
but to keep my eyes on him.

1726
01:11:12,270 --> 01:11:14,500
He put everything into nice words,

1727
01:11:14,500 --> 01:11:16,840
explained the game like a game master,

1728
01:11:16,840 --> 01:11:19,780
and even said,
"I'm also after a particular benefit."

1729
01:11:19,780 --> 01:11:23,410
But how deep his evil side really goes
is something worth keeping an eye on.

1730
01:11:23,410 --> 01:11:25,250
Based on my experience
with survival shows,

1731
01:11:25,250 --> 01:11:26,880
I've learned that someone
who is kind to everyone...

1732
01:11:26,880 --> 01:11:28,550
is someone you should be cautious about.

1733
01:11:29,120 --> 01:11:31,860
One hour has passed
in the trading session.

1734
01:11:31,990 --> 01:11:33,820
Groups wishing to make a trade...

1735
01:11:33,820 --> 01:11:35,930
must have their leader bring
the necessary resources...

1736
01:11:35,930 --> 01:11:38,600
to the Community Center.

1737
01:11:38,900 --> 01:11:41,470
Team Blue, are you free?

1738
01:11:41,470 --> 01:11:43,000
- Yes.
- Good.

1739
01:11:43,000 --> 01:11:45,200
- We were about to...
- Let's have an in-depth conversation.

1740
01:11:45,200 --> 01:11:47,600
- Sure.
- From what I see,

1741
01:11:47,600 --> 01:11:50,110
the diamonds don't hold the power of life.

1742
01:11:50,110 --> 01:11:52,840
- That's true.
- Team Blue is who holds that power.

1743
01:11:52,840 --> 01:11:55,680
But our team holds the right to trade.

1744
01:11:55,680 --> 01:11:58,520
One of the rules was to come
to an agreement and submit one credit.

1745
01:11:58,920 --> 01:12:00,450
Those who want to trade decide...

1746
01:12:00,450 --> 01:12:02,990
- which team will submit the one credit.
- Right.

1747
01:12:02,990 --> 01:12:05,360
When making the trade,
our team will submit the credit.

1748
01:12:05,360 --> 01:12:07,460
- Okay.
- We'll give you nine credits.

1749
01:12:07,460 --> 01:12:09,090
Please give us 12 lives in return.

1750
01:12:09,090 --> 01:12:10,530
- Sorry?
- Twelve lives.

1751
01:12:10,530 --> 01:12:11,760
With nine credits?

1752
01:12:11,760 --> 01:12:15,170
The ratio is based on all the resources
currently disclosed so far.

1753
01:12:15,170 --> 01:12:18,040
- Our team needs credit to do anything.
- Right.

1754
01:12:18,040 --> 01:12:22,840
But we don't want to secure credits
by going that far.

1755
01:12:22,840 --> 01:12:24,570
- Really?
- Based on what you said earlier,

1756
01:12:24,570 --> 01:12:25,840
we can offer six lives.

1757
01:12:25,840 --> 01:12:27,480
And how many credits in return?

1758
01:12:27,480 --> 01:12:29,380
The same amount mentioned before.

1759
01:12:29,380 --> 01:12:31,110
You're ripping us off.

1760
01:12:31,110 --> 01:12:32,850
Say that...

1761
01:12:32,850 --> 01:12:35,190
- it's one credit per team.
- Okay.

1762
01:12:35,190 --> 01:12:37,420
The value of one credit
might not be the same...

1763
01:12:37,420 --> 01:12:39,090
as one life.

1764
01:12:39,120 --> 01:12:40,790
- Of one credit?
- Yes.

1765
01:12:40,790 --> 01:12:42,430
- I see.
- That team calculated it as...

1766
01:12:42,430 --> 01:12:45,830
a percentage of the total amount.

1767
01:12:45,830 --> 01:12:50,330
Each team needs three lives a day
to survive.

1768
01:12:50,770 --> 01:12:54,140
But 1 credit is needed
for a 1-time trade...

1769
01:12:54,140 --> 01:12:57,010
involving six people.

1770
01:12:57,440 --> 01:13:01,810
So, for example, that means
1 credit is worth 1-6th of a life.

1771
01:13:02,310 --> 01:13:04,080
But the amount used per day...

1772
01:13:04,210 --> 01:13:05,720
is clearly different, no?

1773
01:13:05,780 --> 01:13:08,450
- Life...
- Three lives are used for sure.

1774
01:13:08,450 --> 01:13:09,720
- But not credits.
- Three lives are used every day,

1775
01:13:09,720 --> 01:13:11,490
but more credits could be used than that.

1776
01:13:11,990 --> 01:13:13,690
- Yes.
- True, but that's currently unclear.

1777
01:13:13,690 --> 01:13:15,390
Since we're not sure about anything,

1778
01:13:16,360 --> 01:13:18,030
how about we trade tomorrow
after we find out more?

1779
01:13:23,670 --> 01:13:24,670
- Tomorrow?
- Yes.

1780
01:13:24,770 --> 01:13:29,070
You're talking about
10 credits for 6 lives.

1781
01:13:29,540 --> 01:13:30,570
Correct.

1782
01:13:30,640 --> 01:13:31,640
It feels wrong.

1783
01:13:31,640 --> 01:13:32,640
- I agree.
- We believe credits...

1784
01:13:32,640 --> 01:13:34,080
- have more value than that.
- Or...

1785
01:13:34,080 --> 01:13:35,810
How about 9 credits for 6 lives?

1786
01:13:35,810 --> 01:13:38,980
- Instead, we'll submit the one credit.
- Well...

1787
01:13:38,980 --> 01:13:41,390
The offer we can make is
for our team to submit the one credit...

1788
01:13:41,390 --> 01:13:43,520
and trade 5 credits for 6 lives.

1789
01:13:43,520 --> 01:13:44,650
- The same amount?
- Yes.

1790
01:13:44,650 --> 01:13:46,190
- We'd be trading six each in total.
- Yes.

1791
01:13:46,190 --> 01:13:47,260
If you can't agree to that,

1792
01:13:47,460 --> 01:13:49,390
we first want to see
how valuable credit is.

1793
01:13:49,390 --> 01:13:51,730
- You want to check first?
- Credits are the least abundant...

1794
01:13:51,730 --> 01:13:53,000
among the resources.

1795
01:13:53,000 --> 01:13:54,560
- Trading six each...
- Yes?

1796
01:13:54,560 --> 01:13:57,430
Let us discuss whether
we're willing to make such a trade...

1797
01:13:57,430 --> 01:13:59,140
- with the credits.
- Okay.

1798
01:13:59,140 --> 01:14:01,300
- That's the final offer we can make.
- Got it.

1799
01:14:01,870 --> 01:14:04,910
The rate of consumption may be different
from the rate for life,

1800
01:14:04,910 --> 01:14:07,380
but things felt off
when she wanted to trade...

1801
01:14:07,840 --> 01:14:09,010
six lives for ten credits.

1802
01:14:09,580 --> 01:14:11,980
I thought,
"We shouldn't back down anymore."

1803
01:14:11,980 --> 01:14:13,680
"If we accept such an offer,"

1804
01:14:13,680 --> 01:14:17,250
"everything might end up going the way
Team Blue wants."

1805
01:14:17,650 --> 01:14:19,720
I no longer wanted to make the trade.

1806
01:14:20,090 --> 01:14:21,160
Among the members of Team Red,

1807
01:14:21,160 --> 01:14:23,290
it's hard to tell
what Maillard is thinking.

1808
01:14:23,630 --> 01:14:25,600
He's not giving off a nice vibe, you know?

1809
01:14:25,660 --> 01:14:27,560
We might have a little trouble with him.

1810
01:14:29,070 --> 01:14:31,500
- I think doing a trade...
- About six?

1811
01:14:31,500 --> 01:14:34,340
- isn't that bad an idea.
- Six seems fine.

1812
01:14:34,700 --> 01:14:36,410
- It's just that...
- Yes?

1813
01:14:36,410 --> 01:14:39,280
my mean side won't die down.

1814
01:14:39,280 --> 01:14:42,280
I can't stand people
who hold our lifeline hostage...

1815
01:14:42,280 --> 01:14:43,580
and try to control it.

1816
01:14:44,080 --> 01:14:46,150
If only I could be like,
"Let's go down together."

1817
01:14:46,520 --> 01:14:48,650
- But I should calm down.
- For now.

1818
01:14:48,650 --> 01:14:50,150
Even if the trade falls through,

1819
01:14:50,150 --> 01:14:53,060
we could still survive tomorrow for now.

1820
01:14:53,060 --> 01:14:58,160
The value of credits might go up tomorrow.

1821
01:14:58,160 --> 01:15:01,700
Maillard said it with much confidence,

1822
01:15:01,700 --> 01:15:03,730
which we all agreed on.

1823
01:15:04,100 --> 01:15:06,740
You two give me reassurance.

1824
01:15:07,170 --> 01:15:08,370
- What?
- You know.

1825
01:15:08,370 --> 01:15:09,640
- If...
- Yes?

1826
01:15:10,110 --> 01:15:12,380
If our team only had three people like me,

1827
01:15:12,540 --> 01:15:16,050
I don't think we could've pulled off
a deal this bold.

1828
01:15:16,150 --> 01:15:18,380
I couldn't even have made such an offer.

1829
01:15:19,050 --> 01:15:21,220
- Hi.
- Yes, hi.

1830
01:15:21,220 --> 01:15:23,920
- Did you have a good chat with Team Blue?
- Yes.

1831
01:15:23,920 --> 01:15:25,720
- Trade...
- We're still talking.

1832
01:15:25,720 --> 01:15:27,560
More so because they have the lifeline.

1833
01:15:27,560 --> 01:15:29,790
- True, and it's important.
- It's far from easy.

1834
01:15:29,790 --> 01:15:30,890
You have it tough.

1835
01:15:30,890 --> 01:15:31,930
(Breathing in)

1836
01:15:31,930 --> 01:15:33,130
Why aren't you looking at us?

1837
01:15:34,400 --> 01:15:37,370
- We're a little weird about rich people.
- Is that so?

1838
01:15:37,370 --> 01:15:38,600
What's the use of being rich?

1839
01:15:38,600 --> 01:15:39,940
- Sorry?
- We're jealous of you,

1840
01:15:39,940 --> 01:15:41,000
who have many chances.

1841
01:15:41,000 --> 01:15:44,670
Our team just came back from
being bullied about having chances.

1842
01:15:44,670 --> 01:15:46,080
They said the chances weren't necessary.

1843
01:15:46,080 --> 01:15:49,050
- We think there's value in chances.
- Then...

1844
01:15:49,610 --> 01:15:51,250
How's the value of credits?

1845
01:15:51,920 --> 01:15:54,580
We want to secure many credits.

1846
01:15:55,020 --> 01:15:59,660
May we ask how much
you're willing to trade?

1847
01:15:59,660 --> 01:16:02,260
Honestly,
if we give you a bunch right now,

1848
01:16:02,260 --> 01:16:03,560
we can't help but wonder...

1849
01:16:03,560 --> 01:16:05,930
if the value of the diamonds we receive
will be the same tomorrow.

1850
01:16:05,930 --> 01:16:08,470
- The problem lies with trust.
- Correct.

1851
01:16:08,470 --> 01:16:10,330
We did some thinking.

1852
01:16:10,330 --> 01:16:11,740
Why are we Team White?

1853
01:16:12,070 --> 01:16:14,910
We realized money isn't something
we're that savvy with.

1854
01:16:14,910 --> 01:16:16,110
We're innocent and pure.

1855
01:16:16,570 --> 01:16:18,680
Really, this isn't all just nice words.

1856
01:16:18,680 --> 01:16:20,840
We were saying to each other,
"Now I see why we're Team White."

1857
01:16:20,840 --> 01:16:22,350
Everyone on our team is
just so kind-hearted.

1858
01:16:22,350 --> 01:16:23,550
- Is that so?
- So...

1859
01:16:23,550 --> 01:16:25,980
- "Isn't it best to survive together?"
- Nice.

1860
01:16:26,220 --> 01:16:29,350
If we could make a good deal
from the start,

1861
01:16:29,350 --> 01:16:32,020
there would be no reason for us
to devalue the currency.

1862
01:16:32,020 --> 01:16:34,320
Say that we release a lot of credits.

1863
01:16:34,320 --> 01:16:36,590
Would you be willing to keep trading...

1864
01:16:36,590 --> 01:16:37,860
- at the same rate later on?
- Of course.

1865
01:16:37,860 --> 01:16:40,930
That includes a guarantee
that we won't devalue the currency,

1866
01:16:40,930 --> 01:16:43,870
as well as compensation for the favor
you're doing us by making...

1867
01:16:44,100 --> 01:16:45,700
a generous trade in the first place.

1868
01:16:45,700 --> 01:16:48,070
To be frank,

1869
01:16:48,070 --> 01:16:51,510
we wanted to ask for 30 diamonds
for 2 credits.

1870
01:16:51,510 --> 01:16:53,240
- What?
- Two credits...

1871
01:16:53,240 --> 01:16:57,010
Our team won't gain anything
by trading diamonds.

1872
01:16:57,010 --> 01:17:00,150
If that's the case,
we'll need at least six credits.

1873
01:17:00,150 --> 01:17:03,690
- So we'd have to give you 90 diamonds.
- Yes.

1874
01:17:03,690 --> 01:17:04,820
- Then...
- There's no other option.

1875
01:17:04,820 --> 01:17:06,790
- What matters right now is lives.
- Correct.

1876
01:17:06,790 --> 01:17:08,260
The resources we have right now...

1877
01:17:08,260 --> 01:17:10,090
You won't have anything to trade for life.

1878
01:17:10,090 --> 01:17:11,530
- No.
- Right?

1879
01:17:11,530 --> 01:17:12,530
Exactly.

1880
01:17:12,530 --> 01:17:13,560
Then...

1881
01:17:13,560 --> 01:17:15,970
How far are we willing to go
with Team White's rate?

1882
01:17:15,970 --> 01:17:18,000
- One to ten? We went with one to fifteen.
- One to ten? One to fifteen?

1883
01:17:18,000 --> 01:17:19,540
- One to fifteen?
- Sixty diamonds?

1884
01:17:19,540 --> 01:17:20,640
Sixty is...

1885
01:17:20,640 --> 01:17:22,370
Did you come to an agreement?

1886
01:17:22,370 --> 01:17:24,940
We can offer 6 credits for 60 diamonds.

1887
01:17:25,040 --> 01:17:29,550
That's a little more expensive
than we imagined.

1888
01:17:29,550 --> 01:17:31,410
From our perspective,

1889
01:17:31,410 --> 01:17:34,880
we have to pay both sides
to make these purchases,

1890
01:17:34,880 --> 01:17:37,450
and at this point, we believe lives...

1891
01:17:37,450 --> 01:17:39,320
are more valuable than credits.

1892
01:17:39,320 --> 01:17:42,260
How does 50 diamonds sound?

1893
01:17:42,260 --> 01:17:44,130
- Credits are...
- Okay. We're fine with 50.

1894
01:17:44,130 --> 01:17:45,360
- Nice. Thank you.
- Good.

1895
01:17:45,360 --> 01:17:46,730
- Thank you for the easy deal!
- No problem.

1896
01:17:47,760 --> 01:17:49,100
We'll see you at the Community Center.

1897
01:17:49,100 --> 01:17:50,570
Let's go with that
since we're all in a pinch.

1898
01:17:50,570 --> 01:17:51,940
- Thank you very much.
- Great.

1899
01:17:52,240 --> 01:17:53,670
They made the deal really easy.

1900
01:17:53,670 --> 01:17:55,470
We planned to do a little negotiating,

1901
01:17:55,470 --> 01:17:56,810
but he was like, "Okay."

1902
01:17:59,580 --> 01:18:04,210
When Team White mentioned
wanting to keep a good relationship...

1903
01:18:04,210 --> 01:18:06,720
by making a good trade,

1904
01:18:06,720 --> 01:18:10,250
being stubborn about
our trading conditions...

1905
01:18:10,250 --> 01:18:11,960
didn't look all that beneficial.

1906
01:18:11,960 --> 01:18:14,220
Loosening up a bit
seemed like a good idea.

1907
01:18:14,360 --> 01:18:15,660
Shall we get straight to the point?

1908
01:18:15,660 --> 01:18:17,290
- Yes, let's do that.
- Good.

1909
01:18:17,290 --> 01:18:19,830
- We need lives, as you already know.
- Of course.

1910
01:18:19,830 --> 01:18:23,030
We have a big team,
so we need a handful of lives.

1911
01:18:23,200 --> 01:18:25,840
- How much can you offer?
- Right. You see...

1912
01:18:25,840 --> 01:18:29,570
We thought nine was the maximum,

1913
01:18:29,570 --> 01:18:30,970
but those nine aren't all for Team White.

1914
01:18:30,970 --> 01:18:32,710
Limiting it to nine...

1915
01:18:32,710 --> 01:18:35,210
It's three days' worth of lives for us.

1916
01:18:35,210 --> 01:18:38,850
Three days' worth? Won't you survive
even without the nine lives?

1917
01:18:38,850 --> 01:18:41,150
If you do the math,
it won't last us until the end.

1918
01:18:41,150 --> 01:18:44,050
I'm a little confused about the diamonds.

1919
01:18:44,050 --> 01:18:45,590
They said additional diamonds...

1920
01:18:45,590 --> 01:18:46,790
- will be issued starting Day Two.
- That's right.

1921
01:18:46,790 --> 01:18:49,560
- So the value of one diamond now...
- It'll be different.

1922
01:18:49,560 --> 01:18:52,300
The bottom line here
is that there's no point...

1923
01:18:52,300 --> 01:18:54,560
in us setting the unit price
or the quality ourselves.

1924
01:18:54,560 --> 01:18:56,970
- That's right.
- How many are you going to give us?

1925
01:18:56,970 --> 01:18:58,870
We originally planned
on giving out nine...

1926
01:18:58,870 --> 01:19:01,470
and were discussing how
to allocate them into a 6-3 split.

1927
01:19:01,470 --> 01:19:05,710
Whether we're willing
to give out more than nine is...

1928
01:19:05,710 --> 01:19:08,510
something I'll talk it over
with these two and see.

1929
01:19:08,510 --> 01:19:09,610
Right.

1930
01:19:09,610 --> 01:19:12,120
Team Blue appeared...

1931
01:19:12,120 --> 01:19:17,390
unwilling to give up any lives.

1932
01:19:17,390 --> 01:19:18,990
Based on our calculations,

1933
01:19:18,990 --> 01:19:21,120
it seemed like they had plenty
of lives to last for several days.

1934
01:19:21,120 --> 01:19:24,890
But they took a very defensive stance.

1935
01:19:24,890 --> 01:19:26,200
They won't give it to us so easily.

1936
01:19:26,830 --> 01:19:28,600
It's unsettling.

1937
01:19:28,600 --> 01:19:31,530
But if they insist
on not giving any lives,

1938
01:19:31,530 --> 01:19:34,640
we'll do the same and call it off.

1939
01:19:34,640 --> 01:19:36,540
They'll starve,
and they'll change their minds.

1940
01:19:36,670 --> 01:19:38,940
Either way,
they've already got it in their heads...

1941
01:19:38,940 --> 01:19:40,310
- that we're going to issue them anyway.
- Right.

1942
01:19:40,310 --> 01:19:42,710
- As much as possible...
- That's their assumption.

1943
01:19:42,710 --> 01:19:44,450
- That's right.
- What if they give us all the lives,

1944
01:19:44,450 --> 01:19:46,780
- but not much gets issued?
- Right.

1945
01:19:46,780 --> 01:19:49,250
Will they still be willing
to take that risk?

1946
01:19:49,590 --> 01:19:52,660
That still leaves us with enough
to survive for seven days.

1947
01:19:52,660 --> 01:19:55,390
And since we're more efficient
at generating lives...

1948
01:19:55,390 --> 01:19:57,430
Are you saying we should...

1949
01:19:57,430 --> 01:19:59,000
- give out more lives?
- Not so many.

1950
01:19:59,000 --> 01:20:01,770
This whole situation is too abstract.

1951
01:20:01,770 --> 01:20:04,700
I'm fine with whatever
the two of you decide.

1952
01:20:04,700 --> 01:20:07,570
We can agree
on the overall direction first,

1953
01:20:07,570 --> 01:20:10,240
then we can decide
the numbers accordingly.

1954
01:20:10,240 --> 01:20:12,940
Even during the negotiations,
I felt that the two had different goals...

1955
01:20:12,940 --> 01:20:15,080
and were somewhat at odds with each other.

1956
01:20:15,080 --> 01:20:18,150
I said earlier
that we should give Team White six...

1957
01:20:18,150 --> 01:20:20,480
because I didn't want
to make enemies from the start.

1958
01:20:20,480 --> 01:20:23,520
It doesn't have to be a beautiful picture.

1959
01:20:23,520 --> 01:20:25,290
If we're just looking at the value
of the goods,

1960
01:20:25,290 --> 01:20:27,320
Genie's calculation would make more sense.

1961
01:20:27,320 --> 01:20:28,690
It would be more favorable to our side.

1962
01:20:28,760 --> 01:20:31,530
But the goodwill we gain
by doing things differently...

1963
01:20:31,530 --> 01:20:33,900
is also a really important part
of the value of the exchange for me.

1964
01:20:33,900 --> 01:20:37,330
I can't guarantee it,
but we don't know how it'll be used.

1965
01:20:37,870 --> 01:20:40,340
Actually, I wouldn't say Salt
is completely passive.

1966
01:20:40,340 --> 01:20:43,110
She seemed like someone
who has very strong opinions of her own.

1967
01:20:43,110 --> 01:20:46,810
The vibe she gave off
kind of reminded me of Polar Bear.

1968
01:20:46,810 --> 01:20:50,210
They're the kind of people
with strong convictions.

1969
01:20:50,280 --> 01:20:51,680
What they want...

1970
01:20:51,680 --> 01:20:53,350
is ten for each life.

1971
01:20:54,020 --> 01:20:55,320
I think it's not bad.

1972
01:20:55,320 --> 01:20:57,320
It's 1 for 10,

1973
01:20:57,320 --> 01:20:59,090
- and they'll use the one credit.
- Okay.

1974
01:20:59,090 --> 01:21:00,490
All right.

1975
01:21:01,160 --> 01:21:03,260
- Toward coexisting.
- You can go and make the deal.

1976
01:21:03,260 --> 01:21:04,990
I'll go and talk to Team Red.

1977
01:21:05,700 --> 01:21:07,760
I'm practically running around...

1978
01:21:07,760 --> 01:21:09,630
like a real estate agent.

1979
01:21:09,630 --> 01:21:13,140
In a way, I think I've earned
everyone's trust.

1980
01:21:13,570 --> 01:21:16,570
Two hours have passed
in the trading session.

1981
01:21:16,570 --> 01:21:18,910
I'll head to the dormitory for a bit.

1982
01:21:20,110 --> 01:21:22,110
I really want to use
the credits to make threats.

1983
01:21:22,680 --> 01:21:24,410
I don't want to give out any today.

1984
01:21:24,680 --> 01:21:26,420
- But if we fail,
- Yes.

1985
01:21:26,480 --> 01:21:27,850
- only one person will be left here.
- Right.

1986
01:21:28,590 --> 01:21:29,890
We have to earn some lives.

1987
01:21:30,190 --> 01:21:32,090
- Tomorrow has to come for us to...
- Right.

1988
01:21:32,220 --> 01:21:33,220
have an idea about the values.

1989
01:21:33,220 --> 01:21:35,120
- Tomorrow is going to be hectic.
- Yes.

1990
01:21:35,190 --> 01:21:38,290
Tomorrow, we might regret
what we did today.

1991
01:21:38,530 --> 01:21:41,870
I think we'll be able
to earn something anyway.

1992
01:21:41,870 --> 01:21:45,030
And they'll also be able
to earn credits anyway.

1993
01:21:45,400 --> 01:21:48,770
That's also why
we shouldn't give out lives so easily.

1994
01:21:48,770 --> 01:21:49,910
That's right.

1995
01:21:58,210 --> 01:21:59,380
Goodness.

1996
01:21:59,380 --> 01:22:01,520
- We should take them all, right?
- Yes.

1997
01:22:09,390 --> 01:22:10,630
These are the lives.

1998
01:22:11,430 --> 01:22:12,730
These are worth 200,000 dollars.

1999
01:22:13,930 --> 01:22:16,230
It'll be over
if we just go home with these.

2000
01:22:16,470 --> 01:22:18,500
I have to give out 12.

2001
01:22:19,140 --> 01:22:21,370
Are we sure about giving out 12?

2002
01:22:21,670 --> 01:22:22,770
What do you think?

2003
01:22:22,770 --> 01:22:24,570
Let's settle on it.

2004
01:22:24,570 --> 01:22:25,780
Let's go with 12.

2005
01:22:25,780 --> 01:22:27,340
That way, we can earn things...

2006
01:22:27,410 --> 01:22:29,550
and still do something.

2007
01:22:29,710 --> 01:22:32,850
We've secured our survival
for six days anyway.

2008
01:22:32,850 --> 01:22:34,680
That alone can make us drop our worries.

2009
01:22:35,550 --> 01:22:37,590
Are we going to the Community Center?

2010
01:22:37,590 --> 01:22:38,720
Or do we go to the place we were earlier?

2011
01:22:40,060 --> 01:22:42,160
Team Blue is being way too conservative.

2012
01:22:44,030 --> 01:22:45,130
First,

2013
01:22:45,900 --> 01:22:49,930
we don't know how much
we're capable of producing.

2014
01:22:49,930 --> 01:22:51,130
That worries me the most.

2015
01:22:51,130 --> 01:22:53,540
What if we all turn out to be idiots...

2016
01:22:53,900 --> 01:22:55,710
and keep losing in all the games?

2017
01:22:55,710 --> 01:22:57,370
First of all,

2018
01:22:57,370 --> 01:22:58,470
I'm really...

2019
01:22:58,470 --> 01:23:01,310
not an intellectual person. So...

2020
01:23:02,010 --> 01:23:03,550
Are we sure we can sell these?

2021
01:23:03,580 --> 01:23:05,650
- We have to think about that.
- I'm serious.

2022
01:23:05,650 --> 01:23:07,880
I'm saying maybe it'd be better...

2023
01:23:07,880 --> 01:23:10,120
to send out less and prioritize stability.

2024
01:23:10,120 --> 01:23:13,660
I don't think three lives
would make so much of a difference.

2025
01:23:14,560 --> 01:23:18,160
There are 30 minutes remaining
before the Trading Station closes.

2026
01:23:18,160 --> 01:23:19,560
- So...
- Gosh.

2027
01:23:20,430 --> 01:23:22,170
Is Team Blue talking to Team Red?

2028
01:23:22,170 --> 01:23:24,100
- No.
- Team Red is over there.

2029
01:23:24,100 --> 01:23:25,900
- Where is Team Blue?
- Team Blue isn't around.

2030
01:23:25,900 --> 01:23:27,640
They haven't come yet.
There's not much time left.

2031
01:23:27,870 --> 01:23:29,470
- Did they disappear on us?
- I know, right?

2032
01:23:29,470 --> 01:23:30,570
Like this?

2033
01:23:30,810 --> 01:23:33,840
Let's settle this here.

2034
01:23:33,840 --> 01:23:35,280
We're a team.

2035
01:23:35,280 --> 01:23:38,210
The moment we start blaming
each other for mistakes,

2036
01:23:38,210 --> 01:23:40,150
- the team will fall apart.
- That's right.

2037
01:23:40,150 --> 01:23:42,250
That's right. For example,
the game didn't end well...

2038
01:23:42,250 --> 01:23:45,590
after everything,
and one of us was the reason.

2039
01:23:45,590 --> 01:23:47,360
Still, we earned two lives.

2040
01:23:47,720 --> 01:23:50,060
What do we do in that context?

2041
01:23:50,560 --> 01:23:53,000
- Why is Team Blue...
- Team Blue isn't coming.

2042
01:23:54,630 --> 01:23:55,830
Team Blue!

2043
01:23:58,940 --> 01:24:00,270
- What are they talking about?
- They must be discussing things...

2044
01:24:00,270 --> 01:24:01,570
Isn't there a time limit?

2045
01:24:01,570 --> 01:24:04,070
- We have 30 minutes left.
- We have less than 30 minutes left.

2046
01:24:04,070 --> 01:24:06,310
- Shall we proceed first?
- Should we just go ahead?

2047
01:24:06,310 --> 01:24:07,980
We've decided on what to do.

2048
01:24:07,980 --> 01:24:09,910
Are they trying to drag this out...

2049
01:24:09,910 --> 01:24:11,750
- No way.
- to change the exchange rate?

2050
01:24:11,750 --> 01:24:13,220
They wouldn't do that
starting from the first day.

2051
01:24:13,220 --> 01:24:14,380
I don't think they would.

2052
01:24:14,380 --> 01:24:15,950
But do they have more things
to talk about?

2053
01:24:18,120 --> 01:24:19,190
Twelve lives...

2054
01:24:19,660 --> 01:24:21,460
is quite a lot.

2055
01:24:22,160 --> 01:24:24,860
- It's not something we can ignore.
- It's worth four days for us.

2056
01:24:25,190 --> 01:24:28,630
But shouldn't we invest something
to earn something?

2057
01:24:29,630 --> 01:24:31,430
Shall we trade with Team Red first?

2058
01:24:31,430 --> 01:24:32,500
Let's proceed.

2059
01:24:33,400 --> 01:24:35,740
(Trade between Team White and Team Red:
50 diamonds with 5 credits)

2060
01:24:35,740 --> 01:24:37,740
(Community Center)

2061
01:24:40,210 --> 01:24:42,010
(Team Red pays the one credit.)

2062
01:24:43,780 --> 01:24:46,120
The Trading Station has been activated.

2063
01:24:46,480 --> 01:24:47,950
White and red.

2064
01:24:48,820 --> 01:24:50,920
- It was 5 to 50, right?
- Yes.

2065
01:24:52,560 --> 01:24:53,760
Do I just put them here?

2066
01:24:53,760 --> 01:24:54,990
(Exclaiming)

2067
01:24:55,290 --> 01:24:56,690
- This is interesting.
- Awesome.

2068
01:24:57,530 --> 01:24:59,030
(Depositing 50 diamonds)

2069
01:24:59,030 --> 01:25:03,700
(Depositing 5 credits)

2070
01:25:10,270 --> 01:25:11,340
We have to put them all in.

2071
01:25:17,210 --> 01:25:19,180
It'll take some time
because I have to deposit 50 of them.

2072
01:25:19,180 --> 01:25:20,580
Okay. Take your time.

2073
01:25:30,960 --> 01:25:32,300
I'm done.

2074
01:25:36,270 --> 01:25:38,230
We're checking the submitted amount.

2075
01:25:38,530 --> 01:25:40,570
Please wait a moment.

2076
01:25:45,540 --> 01:25:47,340
The trade has been completed.

2077
01:25:47,540 --> 01:25:50,250
Please collect your items and exit.

2078
01:25:56,750 --> 01:25:58,890
- Thank you. Great work.
- Thank you!

2079
01:25:59,360 --> 01:26:01,360
Let's keep making...

2080
01:26:01,990 --> 01:26:03,030
good trades together.

2081
01:26:03,030 --> 01:26:04,230
(Team White and Team Red's trade
has been completed.)

2082
01:26:04,230 --> 01:26:05,460
We don't have time.

2083
01:26:05,760 --> 01:26:07,900
If there are no objections, let's go.

2084
01:26:10,070 --> 01:26:11,230
- Shall we go?
- Yes.

2085
01:26:11,230 --> 01:26:12,340
Six each?

2086
01:26:12,340 --> 01:26:13,700
(They decided to distribute 12 lives.)

2087
01:26:13,700 --> 01:26:14,740
Let's go.

2088
01:26:15,040 --> 01:26:17,240
They're coming. That's a relief.

2089
01:26:17,470 --> 01:26:19,310
We met earlier,
and the rest went to trade.

2090
01:26:19,310 --> 01:26:21,180
- We've been waiting right in front.
- Okay.

2091
01:26:21,180 --> 01:26:23,750
What if the numbers change later?

2092
01:26:23,750 --> 01:26:26,550
- I know, right?
- "One diamond has been deposited."

2093
01:26:26,750 --> 01:26:28,050
What is this?

2094
01:26:28,220 --> 01:26:30,290
- Shall we go and trade, Team White?
- Okay!

2095
01:26:30,820 --> 01:26:32,720
How many did you trade with that team?

2096
01:26:34,090 --> 01:26:36,360
- Is it a secret?
- Yes. I have to keep it like that.

2097
01:26:36,660 --> 01:26:39,200
We can deposit the credit here.

2098
01:26:39,200 --> 01:26:40,630
- We agreed that we'd pay.
- Yes.

2099
01:26:40,630 --> 01:26:41,730
(Team White pays the one credit.)

2100
01:26:42,770 --> 01:26:45,770
- The Trading Station has been activated.
- Thank you.

2101
01:26:48,070 --> 01:26:50,570
- They're six, right?
- Yes.

2102
01:26:50,570 --> 01:26:51,940
(Submitting 6 lives,
submitting 60 diamonds)

2103
01:26:58,080 --> 01:26:59,580
Even if I lie and say I only put one life,

2104
01:26:59,580 --> 01:27:01,380
- it can be checked right away.
- That's when things turn bloody.

2105
01:27:01,720 --> 01:27:04,450
- You can push the person right here.
- That's why it's there.

2106
01:27:04,450 --> 01:27:05,890
For us to fight right here.

2107
01:27:08,930 --> 01:27:10,690
The trade has been completed.

2108
01:27:14,460 --> 01:27:16,200
I feel rich.

2109
01:27:16,200 --> 01:27:18,870
- You have such a big team.
- Right.

2110
01:27:19,500 --> 01:27:21,340
We should really put all our efforts
into producing.

2111
01:27:21,340 --> 01:27:23,440
We'd earn enough to get by day to day.

2112
01:27:23,440 --> 01:27:24,910
That's why we have many members.

2113
01:27:24,910 --> 01:27:26,010
That's right.

2114
01:27:26,010 --> 01:27:27,480
What they want in the end...

2115
01:27:27,480 --> 01:27:30,750
is to do a 1-to-1 after taxes.

2116
01:27:30,750 --> 01:27:32,880
I asked if five would be okay,

2117
01:27:32,880 --> 01:27:33,920
and they said it was.

2118
01:27:34,050 --> 01:27:35,380
Is life worth that much?

2119
01:27:37,350 --> 01:27:39,390
Actually, 5 or 6
doesn't make much of a difference.

2120
01:27:39,460 --> 01:27:41,460
Let's use one less card.
We can profit from this.

2121
01:27:41,460 --> 01:27:42,460
- Okay.
- Okay.

2122
01:27:42,630 --> 01:27:44,690
We were nervous right until we came out,

2123
01:27:44,690 --> 01:27:47,830
and we kept talking about whether
we should give out a little less.

2124
01:27:47,830 --> 01:27:50,130
(Trade between Team Red and Team Blue:
5 credits with 5 lives)

2125
01:27:52,070 --> 01:27:53,640
(Team Red pays the one credit.)

2126
01:27:54,540 --> 01:27:56,940
The Trading Station has been activated.

2127
01:27:56,940 --> 01:27:59,140
- It's 5 with 5, right?
- Yes.

2128
01:27:59,140 --> 01:28:00,310
(Submitting 5 lives, submitting 5 credits)

2129
01:28:01,080 --> 01:28:02,950
We're checking the submitted amount.

2130
01:28:03,350 --> 01:28:05,210
Please wait a moment.

2131
01:28:08,220 --> 01:28:10,050
The trade has been completed.

2132
01:28:12,020 --> 01:28:13,460
- They're coming.
- They're coming.

2133
01:28:13,460 --> 01:28:14,660
Did you succeed in making a deal?

2134
01:28:14,790 --> 01:28:16,030
We made a smooth deal.

2135
01:28:16,030 --> 01:28:17,060
(All trading for Day 1 has ended.)

2136
01:28:17,060 --> 01:28:18,090
- Yes!
- Yes!

2137
01:28:18,360 --> 01:28:19,930
At first, we kept having...

2138
01:28:19,930 --> 01:28:21,800
conflicts around the number of lives.

2139
01:28:21,800 --> 01:28:24,500
But I was worried
people might want to get rid of me...

2140
01:28:24,500 --> 01:28:26,870
if I acted too greedily
from the first day.

2141
01:28:27,240 --> 01:28:28,770
So I tried to be nice,

2142
01:28:29,740 --> 01:28:30,740
but I'm not sure.

2143
01:28:30,740 --> 01:28:32,440
Was I nice?

2144
01:28:32,680 --> 01:28:35,710
No one knows the value of the credits yet.

2145
01:28:35,710 --> 01:28:37,510
So I feel like if we'd been
a little bolder,

2146
01:28:37,510 --> 01:28:39,750
we might have hit it big tomorrow.

2147
01:28:39,950 --> 01:28:44,220
But I think securing
the minimum level of lives...

2148
01:28:44,220 --> 01:28:45,520
was a necessary thing to do.

2149
01:28:45,520 --> 01:28:47,420
We may have been a bit generous today,

2150
01:28:47,420 --> 01:28:50,260
but I think we need to be a little firm.

2151
01:28:50,260 --> 01:28:53,000
Either way, the currency will be issued.

2152
01:28:53,000 --> 01:28:56,270
So we're planning...

2153
01:28:56,270 --> 01:28:57,370
to make it clear
that we have more leverage...

2154
01:28:58,300 --> 01:29:00,870
than they think.

2155
01:29:00,940 --> 01:29:02,070
Should we leave them in our dormitories?

2156
01:29:02,070 --> 01:29:03,670
Does anyone smoke?

2157
01:29:03,670 --> 01:29:05,370
- I smoke.
- That's a relief.

2158
01:29:05,370 --> 01:29:07,010
They have a connection now.

2159
01:29:07,010 --> 01:29:08,210
- Smoking connection.
- Yes.

2160
01:29:08,510 --> 01:29:10,010
- So this is the hub.
- Yes.

2161
01:29:10,010 --> 01:29:12,750
- I guess we can talk here.
- That's right.

2162
01:29:13,580 --> 01:29:15,220
It's peaceful now.

2163
01:29:15,820 --> 01:29:18,150
It's peaceful on the first day.
Starting from the second day...

2164
01:29:18,150 --> 01:29:19,360
(Sighing)

2165
01:29:19,360 --> 01:29:21,420
It's going to get crazy from tomorrow.

2166
01:29:21,420 --> 01:29:23,790
- There'll be cracks from tomorrow.
- Right.

2167
01:29:23,790 --> 01:29:24,960
I'm a bit scared.

2168
01:29:24,960 --> 01:29:26,560
Since we have many members,

2169
01:29:26,560 --> 01:29:29,700
some of us are bound to be eliminated.

2170
01:29:29,700 --> 01:29:30,800
Right.

2171
01:29:30,800 --> 01:29:33,470
Only Vector and I were smokers.

2172
01:29:33,470 --> 01:29:35,840
I was surprised that no one else smoked.

2173
01:29:35,840 --> 01:29:38,870
I'm actually worried...

2174
01:29:38,870 --> 01:29:41,310
that people might think...

2175
01:29:41,310 --> 01:29:43,110
we're plotting something together.

2176
01:29:43,110 --> 01:29:46,320
On the other hand, I think I could get
information from Black...

2177
01:29:46,380 --> 01:29:48,950
and communicate with him.

2178
01:29:50,690 --> 01:29:52,220
- Are you done?
- Yes. Shall we leave?

2179
01:29:52,220 --> 01:29:54,060
- I'll smoke a little longer.
- Okay.

2180
01:29:54,360 --> 01:29:56,130
- You're smoking two in a row.
- Yes.

2181
01:29:56,490 --> 01:29:57,960
- I couldn't smoke earlier.
- Right.

2182
01:30:00,830 --> 01:30:02,330
I actually don't smoke,

2183
01:30:02,630 --> 01:30:05,670
but I forced myself to practice smoking.

2184
01:30:06,040 --> 01:30:08,500
I got there,
and there was only one smoker.

2185
01:30:08,770 --> 01:30:10,840
In that case,

2186
01:30:11,110 --> 01:30:13,840
it's more important to talk
to the other 11 people.

2187
01:30:13,880 --> 01:30:16,380
But it'd be weird to suddenly say...

2188
01:30:16,380 --> 01:30:18,810
that I quit smoking
after smoking on the first day.

2189
01:30:19,220 --> 01:30:20,850
So I'm in a very weird situation.

2190
01:30:22,020 --> 01:30:24,050
I wish someone would just carry me.

2191
01:30:24,050 --> 01:30:25,390
I know, right? It's too high up.

2192
01:30:25,390 --> 01:30:26,490
(Team Red and Team Blue
are climbing up to their dormitories.)

2193
01:30:26,490 --> 01:30:27,890
- This is exercise.
- It's a good workout.

2194
01:30:27,890 --> 01:30:30,360
This isn't fair for those
who don't exercise.

2195
01:30:32,130 --> 01:30:33,400
Team White's dormitory is really big.

2196
01:30:34,260 --> 01:30:36,570
But to go there...

2197
01:30:36,570 --> 01:30:38,570
I know, right? It's too far.

2198
01:30:38,670 --> 01:30:39,870
That's really tough.

2199
01:30:39,940 --> 01:30:41,740
- That would be a real workout.
- Right.

2200
01:30:43,370 --> 01:30:44,510
(Exclaiming)

2201
01:30:44,510 --> 01:30:46,040
- I'm tired.
- Seriously.

2202
01:30:47,480 --> 01:30:48,640
You did so well today.

2203
01:30:48,640 --> 01:30:49,780
- Really?
- Yes.

2204
01:30:50,080 --> 01:30:52,010
- Thank you.
- I could trust you.

2205
01:30:52,310 --> 01:30:55,950
You were also good at playing
a little hard to get in negotiations.

2206
01:30:56,520 --> 01:30:58,650
- We're here.
- Goodness.

2207
01:31:00,890 --> 01:31:02,860
Our important lives.

2208
01:31:07,530 --> 01:31:08,600
Please.

2209
01:31:08,600 --> 01:31:10,300
(Team White's Resources:
90 diamonds, 14 lives, 7 credits)

2210
01:31:10,300 --> 01:31:11,670
Don't let the values drop.

2211
01:31:11,670 --> 01:31:13,940
(Team White's Resources:
90 diamonds, 14 lives, 7 credits)

2212
01:31:14,170 --> 01:31:16,240
- How many do we have? Sixty?
- We have sixty diamonds.

2213
01:31:17,070 --> 01:31:19,280
I wonder if they traded 1-to-1 too.

2214
01:31:20,240 --> 01:31:21,740
I hope we didn't make a losing deal.

2215
01:31:21,980 --> 01:31:23,980
I don't think we did.

2216
01:31:23,980 --> 01:31:25,710
The current price is...

2217
01:31:26,220 --> 01:31:28,650
They said it was 1,000 dollars each.

2218
01:31:28,780 --> 01:31:29,850
That's 60,000 dollars.

2219
01:31:29,850 --> 01:31:31,120
- That's a lot.
- It's 60,000 dollars.

2220
01:31:33,220 --> 01:31:34,860
I feel satisfied with 50 diamonds.

2221
01:31:34,860 --> 01:31:37,790
(Team Red's Resources:
50 diamonds, 9 lives, 18 credits)

2222
01:31:38,430 --> 01:31:39,930
It's dinner time.

2223
01:31:40,660 --> 01:31:44,730
All residents, please gather
at the Town Hall for dinner.

2224
01:31:46,070 --> 01:31:47,240
(Team Black's supermarket is open!)

2225
01:31:47,240 --> 01:31:48,500
So this is how it looks.

2226
01:31:48,740 --> 01:31:50,270
There's a storage place.

2227
01:31:51,010 --> 01:31:52,410
There's jjajang...

2228
01:31:52,680 --> 01:31:54,080
and yukgaejang.

2229
01:31:54,080 --> 01:31:55,410
- Ramyeon!
- There's ramyeon!

2230
01:31:55,410 --> 01:31:56,680
- Who wants ramyeon?
- Pick what you want.

2231
01:31:56,680 --> 01:31:58,910
- There's a variety.
- We have many cup ramyeons.

2232
01:31:59,250 --> 01:32:00,480
That's very...

2233
01:32:01,750 --> 01:32:03,190
You look very rich.

2234
01:32:03,590 --> 01:32:04,790
Ramyeon.

2235
01:32:04,790 --> 01:32:06,160
- That's so cool.
- I'm so jealous.

2236
01:32:06,160 --> 01:32:07,590
- So cool. Right.
- You look like someone with power.

2237
01:32:07,590 --> 01:32:09,360
- He looks wealthy.
- That's right.

2238
01:32:09,990 --> 01:32:11,790
Eight boxed meals...

2239
01:32:12,090 --> 01:32:14,200
and four fried chickens?

2240
01:32:14,600 --> 01:32:16,430
A special meal has been ordered.

2241
01:32:16,730 --> 01:32:17,900
It's on its way,

2242
01:32:17,900 --> 01:32:19,070
- A special meal?
- so please wait a moment.

2243
01:32:19,070 --> 01:32:21,640
- A special meal is on its way.
- It's on me today.

2244
01:32:21,640 --> 01:32:24,040
The boss.

2245
01:32:26,340 --> 01:32:28,210
They're giving us fried chicken
from Day One.

2246
01:32:28,580 --> 01:32:29,980
You have to try it to keep wanting it.

2247
01:32:31,110 --> 01:32:32,150
Right.

2248
01:32:32,850 --> 01:32:33,880
(After fried chicken,
then comes the pork belly.)

2249
01:32:33,880 --> 01:32:34,980
It's pork belly!

2250
01:32:35,350 --> 01:32:37,990
Help us with the meat, Maillard.

2251
01:32:38,050 --> 01:32:39,620
The grilling expert is here.

2252
01:32:39,620 --> 01:32:40,760
That's Maillard.

2253
01:32:40,760 --> 01:32:42,360
The Maillard master.

2254
01:32:42,360 --> 01:32:43,660
He's really living up to his name.

2255
01:32:43,760 --> 01:32:46,060
I'm really curious to see
what Maillard's meat tastes like.

2256
01:32:46,060 --> 01:32:47,700
It's just grilling them in a frying pan.

2257
01:32:47,960 --> 01:32:49,870
Can't everyone in Korea cook this?

2258
01:32:49,870 --> 01:32:51,330
The oil...

2259
01:32:52,400 --> 01:32:53,740
There's pizza here.

2260
01:32:54,470 --> 01:32:56,210
That looks good. It looks really good.

2261
01:32:56,540 --> 01:32:58,210
There's a real expert vibe.

2262
01:32:59,910 --> 01:33:01,110
It looks good.

2263
01:33:01,110 --> 01:33:02,950
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

2264
01:33:03,080 --> 01:33:04,180
Well done.

2265
01:33:04,410 --> 01:33:06,620
- Well done.
- Take a seat.

2266
01:33:06,620 --> 01:33:07,880
Shall we clink with the pork belly?

2267
01:33:07,880 --> 01:33:10,550
- Okay!
- Dumplings and pork belly clink.

2268
01:33:11,290 --> 01:33:12,350
All right.

2269
01:33:12,350 --> 01:33:13,990
- Enjoy your meal.
- Enjoy your meal.

2270
01:33:13,990 --> 01:33:15,390
- Cheers.
- Cheers.

2271
01:33:18,090 --> 01:33:19,430
The pork belly tastes great.

2272
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:21,460
There's mustard in the pizza?

2273
01:33:22,300 --> 01:33:23,870
(Exclaiming)

2274
01:33:24,900 --> 01:33:25,970
This is good.

2275
01:33:26,840 --> 01:33:28,900
- He grilled it so well.
- You're really good with meat.

2276
01:33:28,900 --> 01:33:30,310
Thank you.

2277
01:33:34,040 --> 01:33:35,410
Have some soju as well.

2278
01:33:37,080 --> 01:33:39,080
- Thank you.
- Thank you, boss.

2279
01:33:39,580 --> 01:33:40,820
- I'll just drink a little.
- Sure.

2280
01:33:41,450 --> 01:33:42,650
- Since it's the first day.
- Yes.

2281
01:33:43,390 --> 01:33:44,390
There.

2282
01:33:44,390 --> 01:33:45,420
- Do you drink?
- Yes.

2283
01:33:45,420 --> 01:33:46,990
I'm not sure if this is correct...

2284
01:33:50,090 --> 01:33:51,360
(Exclaiming)

2285
01:33:52,900 --> 01:33:54,400
Will you start business
starting lunch tomorrow?

2286
01:33:54,400 --> 01:33:55,400
That's right.

2287
01:33:55,530 --> 01:33:56,800
What about breakfast?

2288
01:33:56,800 --> 01:33:59,800
You have to take some for breakfast today.

2289
01:34:00,500 --> 01:34:02,270
- Yes.
- Let's pack some food.

2290
01:34:02,540 --> 01:34:04,140
- Eggs.
- Things like eggs.

2291
01:34:04,310 --> 01:34:07,280
Starting tomorrow,
you'll have to pay for them.

2292
01:34:07,280 --> 01:34:08,340
Right.

2293
01:34:08,880 --> 01:34:11,210
I think all the food
is going to become really valuable.

2294
01:34:12,180 --> 01:34:14,980
We could roughly estimate
how many we all had today,

2295
01:34:14,980 --> 01:34:16,390
but starting tomorrow,

2296
01:34:16,390 --> 01:34:17,750
- we won't have any idea.
- We wouldn't know.

2297
01:34:18,520 --> 01:34:20,460
It's really hard to put a value on things.

2298
01:34:21,460 --> 01:34:22,860
Only Vector knows.

2299
01:34:23,230 --> 01:34:25,860
I'm the only one
who knows the golden ratio.

2300
01:34:27,660 --> 01:34:29,060
- I envy you.
- Right.

2301
01:34:29,060 --> 01:34:31,030
But it's useless for me.

2302
01:34:31,570 --> 01:34:33,040
I have to keep the balance.

2303
01:34:33,040 --> 01:34:36,110
I'll provide everyone
the same information fairly.

2304
01:34:36,840 --> 01:34:39,510
Since I'm alone, I'll probably
get to do something else separately.

2305
01:34:42,310 --> 01:34:45,650
Then the supermarket owner
will just keep earning money?

2306
01:34:45,650 --> 01:34:47,480
- That's weird?
- Isn't that weird?

2307
01:34:47,480 --> 01:34:48,720
- It's weird.
- It's too good.

2308
01:34:48,720 --> 01:34:50,420
- Right.
- It's just so good.

2309
01:34:50,550 --> 01:34:52,120
There's no way something like that exists.

2310
01:34:52,660 --> 01:34:55,420
Vector is under a lot of suspicion.

2311
01:34:56,030 --> 01:34:58,730
His play style is too nice.

2312
01:34:59,090 --> 01:35:03,800
People who carry this kind of kindness
into a survival game...

2313
01:35:04,530 --> 01:35:05,800
have a slightly different mission.

2314
01:35:06,100 --> 01:35:09,240
So far, Vector has no risks.

2315
01:35:09,410 --> 01:35:11,440
He can just eat whatever he wants,
and when people come,

2316
01:35:11,440 --> 01:35:13,880
he can get diamonds
to earn the prize money.

2317
01:35:13,940 --> 01:35:16,050
If that's how it will end,
it wouldn't make any sense.

2318
01:35:16,480 --> 01:35:17,680
Maybe because it's the first day,

2319
01:35:18,210 --> 01:35:21,320
he let the child taste the candy.

2320
01:35:21,320 --> 01:35:22,450
"Isn't it tasty?"

2321
01:35:22,450 --> 01:35:24,550
He lets us eat so much...

2322
01:35:24,890 --> 01:35:26,660
and tells us to pay from the next day.

2323
01:35:26,960 --> 01:35:28,590
Maybe he was just laying the groundwork.

2324
01:35:28,960 --> 01:35:32,560
He's just training us
like we're a bunch of puppies.

2325
01:35:32,900 --> 01:35:35,460
Puppies are trained using food.

2326
01:35:35,460 --> 01:35:37,400
He fed us tasty meat.

2327
01:35:37,770 --> 01:35:39,940
He's going to tighten the leash now.

2328
01:35:41,940 --> 01:35:43,770
When trades don't work out,

2329
01:35:43,770 --> 01:35:44,810
Yes?

2330
01:35:45,170 --> 01:35:47,040
could things come to blows?

2331
01:35:47,110 --> 01:35:48,110
A real-life fight?

2332
01:35:48,110 --> 01:35:49,240
- A fight...
- A real-life fight?

2333
01:35:49,240 --> 01:35:51,810
- A real-life fight.
- There's a chance of a real-life fight.

2334
01:35:52,350 --> 01:35:53,980
Desperation could lead to that.

2335
01:35:54,050 --> 01:35:55,220
If we get desperate...

2336
01:35:55,220 --> 01:35:56,350
You were cooler than we thought.

2337
01:35:56,350 --> 01:35:58,590
You could have held out
a little longer, right, Team Blue?

2338
01:35:59,020 --> 01:36:00,790
You could have said,

2339
01:36:00,790 --> 01:36:01,890
- "Try holding out until tomorrow."
- We could have done that.

2340
01:36:01,890 --> 01:36:03,230
- We could've said, "Take it or leave it."
- Yes.

2341
01:36:03,230 --> 01:36:04,830
We could just choose not to give them out.

2342
01:36:04,830 --> 01:36:06,900
We could just let everyone get eliminated.

2343
01:36:06,900 --> 01:36:09,730
That's what we said at first
in a joking manner,

2344
01:36:09,730 --> 01:36:12,870
but we wanted it to go
peacefully on Day One.

2345
01:36:12,870 --> 01:36:14,800
Don't say such harsh words.

2346
01:36:15,300 --> 01:36:17,470
Right. We wanted to survive together
for a long time.

2347
01:36:17,470 --> 01:36:20,280
Yes. We also gave you some things.

2348
01:36:20,280 --> 01:36:21,910
It'd be really sad
when people disappear...

2349
01:36:21,910 --> 01:36:23,810
when we've been bustling around like this.

2350
01:36:23,810 --> 01:36:25,250
- That's right.
- Right.

2351
01:36:26,980 --> 01:36:28,980
These people will be gone by tomorrow.

2352
01:36:28,980 --> 01:36:30,990
You've got to play the game
for what it is.

2353
01:36:30,990 --> 01:36:33,860
How could you call this a game?

2354
01:36:34,060 --> 01:36:36,530
This was your plan all along!

2355
01:36:36,530 --> 01:36:38,430
Your excuse is so lame.

2356
01:36:44,530 --> 01:36:50,310
(Life submission)

2357
01:36:50,310 --> 01:36:51,340
Something's playing.

2358
01:36:51,340 --> 01:36:54,080
I'd like to announce
today's final official event.

2359
01:36:54,380 --> 01:36:56,180
Life submissions will take place...

2360
01:36:56,180 --> 01:36:58,210
- over the next hour.
- Life submission.

2361
01:36:58,310 --> 01:36:59,850
- Life submission?
- Life submission?

2362
01:36:59,850 --> 01:37:02,050
- See you later.
- This is scary.

2363
01:37:02,050 --> 01:37:04,720
Each group's leader is asked
to prepare enough lives...

2364
01:37:04,720 --> 01:37:06,190
for the number of people in their team...

2365
01:37:06,820 --> 01:37:09,530
and submit them to the Community Center.

2366
01:37:10,060 --> 01:37:11,230
Any resident whose life...

2367
01:37:11,230 --> 01:37:14,230
has not been submitted on their behalf
within the allotted time...

2368
01:37:14,230 --> 01:37:15,830
will be immediately removed
from the premises.

2369
01:37:16,330 --> 01:37:19,170
The six of us are one.

2370
01:37:19,170 --> 01:37:20,540
We could submit one life.

2371
01:37:21,440 --> 01:37:22,540
We're one?

2372
01:37:22,540 --> 01:37:23,840
- We're one.
- I agree.

2373
01:37:23,840 --> 01:37:25,670
- That's a good idea.
- Nice.

2374
01:37:25,670 --> 01:37:27,180
- Right?
- That's right.

2375
01:37:27,180 --> 01:37:28,710
- We're one.
- Of course.

2376
01:37:29,110 --> 01:37:31,080
- I'll go and submit our precious lives.
- Okay.

2377
01:37:31,080 --> 01:37:33,320
- I'll go with her.
- Okay.

2378
01:37:33,320 --> 01:37:34,320
Let's go.

2379
01:37:34,480 --> 01:37:35,850
See you.

2380
01:37:36,390 --> 01:37:37,790
The leader has to do...

2381
01:37:37,790 --> 01:37:39,120
- many bothersome things.
- Right.

2382
01:37:39,120 --> 01:37:40,820
It'd be tiresome.

2383
01:37:41,120 --> 01:37:42,890
- Three.
- Thank you for doing that.

2384
01:37:42,890 --> 01:37:43,930
It's all right.

2385
01:37:44,060 --> 01:37:46,160
I'll go and submit these.

2386
01:37:46,960 --> 01:37:48,860
- On their behalf...
- Why are they giving us an hour?

2387
01:37:49,060 --> 01:37:52,570
We might have to fight over this later.

2388
01:37:52,840 --> 01:37:55,770
For example, if we have two left,
we have to decide...

2389
01:37:55,770 --> 01:37:57,070
- who will be eliminated.
- You...

2390
01:37:57,440 --> 01:37:58,710
You could take these three...

2391
01:37:59,470 --> 01:38:03,380
If you decide not to keep us alive
and submit only one...

2392
01:38:03,380 --> 01:38:05,480
If I submit only one,
the two of you will die?

2393
01:38:05,780 --> 01:38:07,050
This is a bit...

2394
01:38:07,380 --> 01:38:08,780
- Is that what it is?
- Well...

2395
01:38:09,380 --> 01:38:11,690
- Should I come with you?
- No.

2396
01:38:11,920 --> 01:38:12,990
We trust you.

2397
01:38:13,460 --> 01:38:15,320
You won't be able to play the games alone.

2398
01:38:15,320 --> 01:38:16,690
- We can pack our bags.
- It'll be tough.

2399
01:38:27,670 --> 01:38:28,940
If I only submit mine,

2400
01:38:31,070 --> 01:38:32,270
I'd be killing the rest of my team.

2401
01:38:35,380 --> 01:38:36,510
If there's only one person,

2402
01:38:37,680 --> 01:38:39,010
they won't need any lives.

2403
01:38:52,560 --> 01:38:53,700
- If the leader...
- Yes?

2404
01:38:54,660 --> 01:38:56,030
- submits only one life,
- Yes.

2405
01:38:56,570 --> 01:38:57,770
the other two will die, right?

2406
01:38:59,030 --> 01:39:01,070
They said we wouldn't need lives
if only one team member was left.

2407
01:39:03,470 --> 01:39:05,610
Gosh! That's true.

2408
01:39:08,380 --> 01:39:10,810
I suddenly felt more powerful.

2409
01:39:11,010 --> 01:39:15,420
The leader can decide
whether or not to keep someone alive...

2410
01:39:15,980 --> 01:39:18,150
during the submission.

2411
01:39:18,590 --> 01:39:19,920
Being a leader...

2412
01:39:20,590 --> 01:39:22,220
is quite important.

2413
01:39:33,040 --> 01:39:35,070
(Life Submission for Day 1)

2414
01:39:35,070 --> 01:39:37,270
(Donkey, Maillard, Izaha)

2415
01:39:37,470 --> 01:39:39,440
Please check the contents
of the central box...

2416
01:39:40,010 --> 01:39:43,750
and assign lives
to the residents of your choice.

2417
01:39:47,920 --> 01:39:50,450
(Life Submission for Day 1)

2418
01:39:50,450 --> 01:39:51,550
If I only submit mine,

2419
01:39:53,690 --> 01:39:54,960
I'd be killing the rest of my team.

2420
01:39:56,590 --> 01:40:00,530
(Maillard)

2421
01:40:01,000 --> 01:40:03,530
If there's only one person,
they won't need any lives.

2422
01:40:05,470 --> 01:40:09,070
(Life Submission for Day 1)

2423
01:40:12,570 --> 01:40:16,380
(Maillard)

2424
01:40:23,250 --> 01:40:26,460
(The Community 2: Invisible Hand)

2425
01:41:01,720 --> 01:41:03,590
(The Community 2: Invisible Hand)
